## Reading Alongside the Three Factions

A. Outside machines are not a common enemy

Outside machines are autonomous machines that the military and civilians used before the Great Blackout. They handled logistics, inspection, care, guard posts, rail maintenance, swarm drones, and construction, and their makers are gone. They carry out their last orders and repeat the same patrols, and they read light and heat above ground as an anomaly and approach it. This is what the light taboo, "it comes when seen", refers to (outside machines and ancient weapons). Weak in the cold, they wake in a long daylight or near human warmth, and since they cannot be built anew their number only shrinks. The Arc can switch some of them on and off. The AI is not a common enemy of the three factions; to a person caught in a machine''s routine it is a hazard, and once captured it is a resource that becomes parts or a unit. For a chassis to become a unit it passes through capture (all three factions), hardware repair (Government and Free Cities), signal check and wake-up (Arc), and thought control (Free City doctors). Machine type numbers and units follow [Unit Roster and Tech Tree](/wiki/Unit-Roster-and-Tech-Tree/). The machine type numbers (M1 to M14) are a different series from the "source placement" numbers in the entries below.

| Group compendium | Machine type | Path to a unit |
|---|---|---|
| [G13 · Pincer Arm](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G13/) | M5 logistics robot crew | A-U07 woken logistics robots, woken by the Arc |
| [G14 · Empty Cart](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G14/) | M1 service robot | R-U01 cargo service robot, which anyone can catch |
| [G15 · Care Wheel](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G15/) | M4 care robot | Does not become a unit; its remaining records are read with Arc software |
| [G16 · Girder Spider](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G16/) | M2 inspection robot (four-legged) | R-U02 stabilized quadruped, F-U15 neural-direct quadruped troop |
| [G17 · One-Eye Tower](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G17/) | M6 blockade watch net | A-U08 woken sentry post, switched back on by the Arc |
| [G18 · Fire-Leg Train](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G18/) | M3 track and conduit inspection robot | Does not become a unit; it yields parts to maintain rail units |
| [G25 · Lantern Ant](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G25/) | M12 Lantern Ant | R-U04 searchlight insect-drones |
| [G26 · Iron Dinosaur](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G26/) | M13 Iron Dinosaur | R-U05 hydraulic siege machine |

B. Human groups and the cost structure

The hostile human groups are not enemies from outside the three factions; they are bands that formed in the gaps the factions'' cost structure leaves. The reasons are set out in [The Cost Structure of the Three Factions](/wiki/Cost-Structure/).

| Group compendium | Ground | Place in the cost structure |
|---|---|---|
| [G07 · Yongsan Raiders](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G07/) | Government | People who hold no station go after food. The Government wins, but badly wounded soldiers do not come back |
| [G08 · Dongdaemun Raiders](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G08/) | Government | They take the emergency supplies of the doctorless White Cross |
| [G09 · Sangam Deserters](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G09/) | Government | Soldiers who left the Government army. With no officers or doctors, what they hold can only shrink |
| [G10 · Bogukmun group](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G10/) | Government (Bogukmun), Arc (Garak, Jamsil) | They go after grain and distributed seed. Only the Government holds the original seed stock |
| [G11 · Gangnam Stray Humanoids](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G11/) | Arc | Synthetics running on basic functions without a signature. Repairs need Government or Free City hands |
| [G12 · Sinnae Deserters](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G12/) | Government | Soldiers who left the Government army. A faction that takes them keeps them at the very bottom |

C. Winter animals

Winter animals are not mutated creatures but ordinary wild animals that come down to station entrances, vents, and surface floors looking for food and warmth in the artificial winter. The dangers are raids on food stores, carrying disease, and getting in through gaps in a shutter. Residents on low surface floors meet them before residents behind a deep shutter do.

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0004"></a>

## G01 · Wild Boar

Wild boars are ordinary wild animals that live on the slopes of Bukhansan to the north, on the foothills of Gwanaksan and Umyeonsan to the south, and in the reed beds of Nanji on the Han River. Since the Great Blackout people have withdrawn from the surface, and the line between mountain and riverbank has faded. As the winter drags on there is nothing to eat in the hills, so boars come down toward the stations where people live. There are no mutated individuals and no leader that commands the herd.

- Dossier: [G01 · Wild Boar](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0041"></a>

### G01-SC1 · The First Hoof Outside the Embankment

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: The day after the first snow settles, the prints of a sow and her young appear in front of a grain store outside the embankment on the Nanji riverbank of the Han River.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Mapo, White Cross, surface villagers
- Ecological mechanism: When food is buried under the snow, the sow leads the family group to the places where people put out food waste.
- Choices: Delay the collection yard’s night operation / Open a reed-bed detour with a scent lure / Capture only the sow
- Outcomes: Delaying operations delays the ration too. A detour creates a long-term trail. Taking the sow leaves the young circling the same place for days.
- Moral cost: The people’s ration and the survival of a family group with young collide on the same timetable.
- Source dossier: G01

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0043"></a>

### G01-SC2 · The Price of the Salt Pool

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: When heaps of calcium chloride for de-icing are piled in front of a station, the herd moves its feeding ground to the semi-basement alleys and surface villages in search of salt.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Government and the Free Cities, ordinary citizens, road-clearing workers
- Ecological mechanism: The sow that knows where the salt is leads the family group back to the same spot. Even if the heap is removed the herd does not vanish; it is pushed into a more dangerous village.
- Choices: Leave a deliberately placed salt pool in one spot / Prioritize recovering alley scraps and salt / Lower the density with a hunting permit
- Outcomes: The pool fixes the cost of coexistence. Prioritizing recovery takes labor away from other rations. Hunting makes the remaining animals better at avoiding people.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to keep the danger in a safe place and manage it, or to pass it on to a vulnerable alley.
- Source dossier: G01

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0045"></a>

### G01-SC3 · Right of Passage on the Flood Line

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: A column of porters from outside Seoul passes along the flood line on the Han River levee, and the Bukhansan herd comes down the same road.
- Actors: Government stationmaster, porters and refugees from outside Seoul, White Cross
- Ecological mechanism: In the snow the only dry ground is the road on the levee, so people and boars use the same road. Food crumbs dropped by the porters draw the herd in.
- Choices: Divide the passage hours / Turn the food convoy onto a bait trail toward the reed beds / Close the levee road for a while
- Outcomes: Dividing the hours reduces collisions, but people at the back endure the cold longer. The bait trail uses convoy crews and containers. Closing the road drives the porters to other stations.
- Moral cost: The fastest passage and the safest passage are hard to achieve together.
- Source dossier: G01

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0047"></a>

## G02 · Large-billed Crow

The large-billed crow is the commonest crow in Seoul. It has a heavy bill, a domed forehead, and a sharp mind. In a city thinned out since the Great Blackout, rooftops, pylons, and the steel frames of Han River bridges have become good roosts for crows. In winter, with food short, hundreds gather at a single roost, and by day they fly to station exits, grain stores, and food-waste yards where people put out food. There is no special leader and no individual that commands signals.

- Dossier: [G02 · Large-billed Crow](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0082"></a>

### G02-SC1 · Three Horns

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a winter morning at the overhead-wire crossing in Wangsimni, when a maintenance vehicle sounds its horn three times, the crows at the communal roost make the same sound.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Seongdong, overhead-wire maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Crows learn a repeated sound as a signal that food is coming and spread it. When the sound is heard they gather in front of the grain store.
- Choices: Change the horn sound / Use flags instead of sound / Move the grain store
- Outcomes: Changing the sound raises false alarms for a few days, and the birds soon learn the new one. Flags spread less among the birds. Moving the store takes labor.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to reduce first the safety of the workers or the cost of driving the birds off.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0084"></a>

### G02-SC2 · The False Blackout Alarm

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: After crows peck at insulators and droppings pile up, the electricity in the Daerim workshops cuts out and a blackout alarm sounds.
- Actors: Free City stationmaster in Daerim, workshop technicians, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Damp and droppings cause a short circuit and the alarm sounds, and the crows learn that sound as a signal of food.
- Choices: Replace and clean the insulators first / Switch off the alarm sound for a while / Put an off-limits cord around the pylon
- Outcomes: Replacing the insulators restores electricity but takes labor. Switching the alarm off risks missing a real blackout. The off-limits cord does not stop the birds.
- Moral cost: Keeping the workshops powered collides with the safety of the people given the dangerous pylon work.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0086"></a>

### G02-SC3 · A New Signal on the Bridge

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: After a large roost forms in the inspection openings on a Han River bridge, traffic on the bridge stops every dawn.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the districts the bridge reaches, porters and cart-pullers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Gaps in the bridge steel become a sleeping place out of snow and wind, so crows gather, and food dropped by vehicles draws the birds each morning.
- Choices: Close the inspection openings / Shift the passage hours / Clear the food off the bridge
- Outcomes: Closing the openings shrinks the roost but adds inspection labor. Shifting the hours leaves porters standing longer in the cold. Clearing the food moves the birds to the next bridge.
- Moral cost: Decide who bears the bridge inspection labor and the burden on the next bridge the birds move to.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0088"></a>

## G03 · Feral Dog

Feral dogs are dogs that people once kept and lost, and their puppies. After the Great Blackout, dogs that lost their homes, dogs left behind after guarding building sites and warehouses, and dogs separated from their owners during evacuation met in the ruined streets and formed small packs. They are not mutated and do not send signals; they are ordinary dogs living while avoiding people. Memories of having been tamed remain, so they come close to people, but they fear people as much as they trust them.

- Dossier: [G03 · Feral Dog](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0120"></a>

### G03-SC1 · The Rusted Name Tag

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a snowy morning in the Yongsan market alleys, a dog wearing a rusted collar tag snatches a food sack and bolts.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Yongsan, White Cross, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: A dog that people once kept remembers the place and time of the soup kitchen and returns to the same spot.
- Choices: Hang food sacks high / Fix one gathering place for the dogs / Read the tag to find the owner
- Outcomes: Hanging sacks moves the dogs to other alleys. Gathering them in one place fixes where people get hurt. Reading the tag takes time but the dog may be released.
- Moral cost: Safety of people and keeping the dogs alive collide over which to settle first.
- Source dossier: G03

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0122"></a>

### G03-SC2 · The Band That Remembers the Feeding Cart

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: At the unloading yards of Noryangjin Fish Market, a pack of feral dogs gathers at the hour the feeding cart arrives each time, delaying unloading.
- Actors: Free City stationmaster in Noryangjin, unloading workers, fish-market traders
- Ecological mechanism: Dogs remember the cart’s engine sound and the smell of thawing together with the hour, and when that hour comes the pack gathers.
- Choices: Change the cart’s hour / Keep offal in lidded containers / Move the unloading place
- Outcomes: Changing the hour leaves the dogs wandering for a few days. Using containers reduces food and the dogs go elsewhere. Moving the place costs money.
- Moral cost: Decide who bears the safety of the unloading workers, the burden of market labor, and the risk spreading to the next alley.
- Source dossier: G03

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0126"></a>

### G03-SC3 · Rebinding on the Transfer Stairs

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: On a snowy night, dogs that had scattered gather again under the transfer stairs at Yongsan Station and block the stairs.
- Actors: Government stationmaster of Yongsan Station, White Cross, ordinary citizens transferring
- Ecological mechanism: In the cold several families gather under the stairs, where there is shelter from the wind, and their barking calls still more dogs.
- Choices: Block under the stairs with a temporary partition / Leave blankets and food there to lead them to one place / Cut off night passage
- Outcomes: A partition drives the dogs elsewhere. Gathering them in one place fixes where people get bitten. Cutting off passage slows transfers.
- Moral cost: The safety of people transferring and a living place for dogs shivering in the cold cannot both be provided.
- Source dossier: G03

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0128"></a>

## G04 · Raccoon Dog

The raccoon dog belongs to the dog family, but it climbs trees and digs burrows well, and it is a rare species that rests for long stretches in winter in a state close to deep sleep. Since the Great Blackout, river embankments, drainage pipes, and spaces under manholes in a city with fewer people have become good dens for raccoon dogs. As the artificial winter lengthens, their rest shortens, and they come down to station vents and the basements of surface buildings in search of food. There are no mutated individuals and none that commands a fight.

- Dossier: [G04 · Raccoon Dog](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G04/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0161"></a>

### G04-SC1 · The Leading Indicator Under the Manhole

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: Before sudden thaw makes the water rise, a raccoon dog family moves from under a manhole to a vent room.
- Actors: Free City stationmaster, pipe maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Raccoon dogs sense rising water in their bodies first and move to higher ground. This is a real wild-animal habit.
- Choices: Treat the raccoon dogs’ movement as an early warning / Wait for the pump-room gauge reading / Pre-heat the pumps only when the two differ
- Outcomes: An early warning buys preparation time but leaves responsibility for false alarms. Waiting for the measurement means being late for a sudden inflow. Pre-heating only when the two differ saves fuel but misses short downpours.
- Moral cost: The burden of entrusting public safety to animal movements remains.
- Source dossier: G04

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0163"></a>

### G04-SC2 · Two Families in the Dredging Window

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: On the day pipe sediment is cleared, two raccoon dog families were found using burrows in the same pipe at different times of day.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Seongdong, pipe workers, White Cross
- Ecological mechanism: Several families share the same pipe by taking turns, and if one family disappears a neighboring family takes over the burrow.
- Choices: Clear all the sediment at once / Clear it in sections / Wait for a day the burrows are empty
- Outcomes: Clearing all at once drives families that lost their burrows to the surface market. Sections make the job longer. Waiting for an empty day leaves the pipe blocked longer.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to keep the pipe in order, or to risk families with nowhere to go moving to the market.
- Source dossier: G04

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0165"></a>

### G04-SC3 · The Dry Passage of the Pump Room

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: When a cold snap arrives, people and raccoon dogs share a single dry passage to the pump room.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Free City and the Government, pump maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: When snow and ice block other routes, both raccoon dogs and people converge on a single dry passage.
- Choices: Make a heated shelter in the passage to lead the raccoon dogs / Postpone maintenance / Lay a temporary footing outside the passage
- Outcomes: A shelter gathers the raccoon dogs in one place but disease may spread. Postponing maintenance delays the pump. Footing is used for people’s safety.
- Moral cost: Pump maintenance, people’s evacuation, and a dry place for the raccoon dogs collide in one passage.
- Source dossier: G04

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0169"></a>

## G05 · Brown Rat

The brown rat lives wherever people live. Where there is food, warmth, and a hole to hide in, it spreads. After the Great Blackout, when people went underground to live together, rats also gathered beneath the floors of interchange shopping arcades in stations, in cable trays, and around heating pipes. In the artificial winter the surface is frozen and the stations are warm, so rats target the same places as people. There are no mutated rats and no leader that commands them.

- Dossier: [G05 · Brown Rat](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G05/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0204"></a>

### G05-SC1 · The Second Core Nest

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: Thermal imaging shows only one colony, but traces of young are found on the opposite tray.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Gwangjin, facility maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: With several breeding cores, even if one is eliminated the center of the colony moves to another section.
- Choices: Survey all cores first / Borrow an old cable heat map to cut survey time / Test a one-way exit in one section
- Outcomes: Surveying is safe but opens the passage late. Borrowing a heat map is quick but means handing over passage records. Testing an exit closes one unoccupied section for a long time.
- Moral cost: People who want immediate sanitation and people who want to check there is no harm to other animals split over where to spend the time.
- Source dossier: G05

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0208"></a>

### G05-SC2 · The Error in the Sealing Order

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: When the soup-kitchen-side shutter is closed first, the rat colony is driven up into the cables of the evacuation stairs on the Yeouido side.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Government and the Free City, facility maintenance workers, evacuating people
- Ecological mechanism: Rats learn the sealing order and open a new path in the direction left open.
- Choices: Close again from the back / Stop recovering leftovers and leave bait / Send people on a surface detour for the night
- Outcomes: Closing again reduces contamination but makes the work longer. Bait accepts food contamination. A surface detour empties the cable level but needs warmth and guards.
- Moral cost: Fast passage for evacuating people and reducing contamination and harm to other animals cannot both be achieved.
- Source dossier: G05

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0210"></a>

### G05-SC3 · The Predator of the Open Courtyard

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: After unloading, rats block the interchange hub and a proposal is made to release predatory birds.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Government and the Free City, unloading workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Rats increase at a hub where food and heat concentrate, and introducing birds may shrink the colony but bird collisions and droppings become a new problem.
- Choices: Open a courtyard with markers and monitoring / Use only staged sealing / Split unloading schedules to reduce food
- Outcomes: The courtyard needs people to keep it up for a long time. Staged sealing delays rations. Splitting unloading raises cost.
- Moral cost: The responsibility of shifting one ecological problem into another animal’s predation remains.
- Source dossier: G05

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0214"></a>

## G06 · Grey Heron and Cormorant

Grey herons and cormorants are birds that catch fish at the waterside. The heron stands in shallow water, waits, and spears; the cormorant dives and chases its prey. The wetlands of the Han River, Nanji, and Amsa and the waterways of Seoul are winter resting places for migratory and resident birds. In the artificial winter, when rivers and ponds freeze, food shrinks, and they crowd into drainage ponds that do not freeze, puddles at depots, and channels warmed by geothermal heat. There are no mutated birds and no individual that commands the flock.

- Dossier: [G06 · Grey Heron and Cormorant](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G06/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0250"></a>

### G06-SC1 · Three Functional Groups in the Shallow Water

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: As the river begins to freeze, waterfowl, waders, and raptors all gather at a single unfrozen drainage pond.
- Actors: Arc stationmaster in Gangnam, facility maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: When unfrozen water concentrates in one place, waterfowl use the surface, waders the edges, and raptors the high pylons, so three groups overlap at one pond.
- Choices: Leave the pond’s feeding edges as they are / Deliberately clear one edge to lead the birds there / Fit a cover around the pond
- Outcomes: Fixing the level increases birds and the risk of train collisions. Clearing one side gathers birds there. Covers take labor.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to protect train safety or the unfrozen water where birds rest.
- Source dossier: G06

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0254"></a>

### G06-SC2 · The Birds That Learned the Timetable

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: Every time a train passes on the depot’s inner line, the birds take off and the train stops and inspection drags on.
- Actors: Arc stationmaster in Seocho, train maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Birds learn the repeated train times and quiet inspection hours, and gather beside the tracks or leave according to the schedule.
- Choices: Change train times / Remove perches around the tracks / Slow the trains
- Outcomes: Changing times leaves the birds wandering for a few days before they learn again. Removing perches takes labor. Slowing trains slows operations.
- Moral cost: Decide where to spend time: on train operations or on the burden of inspection labor.
- Source dossier: G06

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0256"></a>

### G06-SC3 · The Tracks of Migratory Week

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: In the week migrants arrive, birds settle on the tracks and pylons and train service stops.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Government, the Free Cities, and Arc; train workers; ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Migrants settle all at once, raptors take the high pylons, and waterfowl and waders gather in the shallow water around the tracks, so three heights overlap on one line.
- Choices: Stop trains during migratory week / Put up temporary netting around the tracks / Make a pool for the birds far from the tracks
- Outcomes: Stopping halts supplies. Netting takes labor. A distant pool draws birds away from the tracks, but people who lose the water complain.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to reduce freight by train or the unfrozen water where birds rest.
- Source dossier: G06

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0258"></a>

## G07 · Yongsan Raiders

The Yongsan Raiders are a band of people who hold no station, gathered on Government ground (Yongsan). A person without a station is an ordinary citizen, and these are the ones who sit out the winter on the high floors outside the shutters. They are after food sacks and sealed boxes, and when food runs short they take a ration worker away and trade the person back for food as ransom.

- Dossier: [G07 · Yongsan Raiders](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G07/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0260"></a>

### G07-SC1 · Sealed Boxes at the Leak Pallets

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a rainy-season night the transfer freight elevator is stopped in the Itaewon passage alley and a sealed Government box stands before the leak pallets. The scouts stand in the row of pallets.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: The duty staff count the boxes at the shaft door. The chief is at the inner pallets and the elite squad holds the box hooks.
- Choices: Leave the elevator car in the lower level, lock the shaft door and set the boxes against the wall. / Pass the boxes out of the alley in the order of the inventory board. / Empty the duty post and leave the boxes where the raiders stand.
- Outcomes: If the first is chosen the boxes are in the lower level and the raiders stand before the locked shaft door. If the second, the boxes are out of the alley with their sealing bands still on. If the third, the boxes are on the pallets and the mouths of the elite squad''s sacks are open.
- Moral cost: After the door is locked, that day''s sack count on the board is lower by the number of boxes. After the boxes are passed on in order, the raiders remain in the alley empty-handed. After the post is left empty, the same number is written in the raiders'' sack column.
- Source dossier: G07

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0262"></a>

### G07-SC2 · The Freight Car on a Thaw Morning

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: On a thaw morning a Government freight car enters the transfer freight elevator shaft with a sealing band on its door. The elite squad stands on the rungs of the ladder.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: Damp on the floor of the car runs on to the leak pallets. The box counts of the inventory board and the ration slip differ, and the chief sets sacks beside the car to the amount of the difference. The ration worker on the car stands by the door.
- Choices: Match the ration slip to the inventory board and lower the difference in boxes to the lower level. / Press the sealing band again and send the car to the upper stopping line of the shaft. / Give up the ration worker and load the difference in boxes into the raiders'' sacks.
- Outcomes: If the first is chosen the difference in boxes is against the lower wall and the two slips agree. If the second, the car is at the upper stopping line and the raiders remain on the rungs. If the third, the ration worker is in the elite squad''s hands and the sack mouths are open.
- Moral cost: After the boxes are lowered, the food sacks of the alley are left on the board as the remainder by the amount of the difference. After the car is sent up, the empty threshold runs on to the number of the next car. After the worker is given up, the ransom is written as a number of sacks and is not erased from the board until the person is released.
- Source dossier: G07

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0264"></a>

### G07-SC3 · Food Sacks in the Refrigerated Car

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: On the night the rainy season turns to the thaw, a Government refrigerated car has been pushed as far as the Itaewon passage alley. The raiders gather before the car door.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: A salt stain runs from the floor of the refrigerated car to the leak pallets. Sealed boxes and food sacks lie in the same row. The chief counts the sacks, the elite squad props the door, and the scouts stand at the alley entrance.
- Choices: Leave the car in the lower level and let the duty staff keep only the sack count on the inventory board. / Pass the sealed boxes out of the alley first and release the sacks in order. / Leave the car door open and set the food sacks on the side where the raiders stand.
- Outcomes: If the first is chosen the car is in the lower level and the sack count is written in the next column of the board. If the second, the sealed boxes are out of the alley and the sack count agrees on one board. If the third, the sacks are before the car door and the mouths of the elite squad''s sacks are open.
- Moral cost: After only the count is kept, the food sacks are in the lower level and that day''s ransom column is written next to that number. After the sealed boxes are passed first, the scouts at the entrance stand in a single line. After the door is left open, the same sack numbers are written in the raiders'' column.
- Source dossier: G07

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0266"></a>

## G08 · Dongdaemun Raiders

The Dongdaemun Raiders are an armed band moving through the lower level of the night-wage corridor on Government ground (Dongdaemun). Stationless people and laid-off laborers are mixed in it. The filters and work lamps they carry off in sacks are emergency supplies of the White Cross.

- Dossier: [G08 · Dongdaemun Raiders](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G08/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0268"></a>

### G08-SC1 · Filters on the Shelf

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On an early-winter night the White Cross filter crates are before the positive-pressure doors of the Dongdaemun night-wage corridor and the raiders stand before the crates. The Government patrol duty officer is beside the differential-pressure board of the same door.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: The chief reads the differential-pressure figures and the elite squad takes the crate cords. While the filters are on the shelf the positive-pressure doors stay shut toward the outside.
- Choices: The patrol duty officer moves the filter crates to the inner shelf of the sealed side chamber on the Jegi-dong side. / The White Cross duty officer writes the crate count half on the shelf and half on the raiders'' sacks. / Give up a carrier as the ransom and keep the crates.
- Outcomes: If the crates are moved to the shelf, the raiders stand before the doors and the positive pressure holds. If the count is split in halves, the crates are divided between the shelf and the sacks and the elite squad stands in the alley. If a carrier is given up, the crates stay on the shelf and the carrier is in the elite squad''s hands.
- Moral cost: After the count is divided in halves, the White Cross protection filters remain as a single layer. After a carrier is given up, one patient transfer goes unstaffed and, until release, another carrier bears that share of the work.
- Source dossier: G08

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0270"></a>

### G08-SC2 · Curtains of the Night Wage

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: On a night when the White Cross night-wage duty officer is before the entrance curtains, the scouts stand at the end of the lower windbreak alley. The Government patrol duty officer is beside the differential-pressure board of the same entrance.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: The scouts count the curtain layers and write them on a board, and the chief sets the place of the night-wage sacks before the doors. The patrol duty officer leaves the differential-pressure figures on the record board.
- Choices: The night-wage duty officer keeps the curtains at three layers and opens the night-wage passage toward the inner side chamber. / The patrol duty officer writes the differential-pressure figures on both the chief''s board and the duty record board. / Move the night-wage sacks onto the side-chamber shelf and leave the seized first-aid worker on the chief''s board.
- Outcomes: If the passage is opened, the elite squad stands outside the curtains and the night-wage goods are on the side-chamber side. If the figures are written on both boards, the chief and the duty officer stand before the same board and the sacks remain at the doors. If the sacks are moved, the scouts are at the duct mouth and the first-aid worker remains as an item on the chief''s board.
- Moral cost: Where the passage is opened, the night-wage goods remain in the sack count, and where the figures are written together the duty officer spends the night beside the door. While the first-aid worker is held, the White Cross is short one first-aid team.
- Source dossier: G08

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0272"></a>

### G08-SC3 · Dust at the Entrance

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: On a night when dust from the relay dock is piled at the entrance of the Dongdaemun night-wage corridor, a higher figure is written on the entrance differential-pressure board. The White Cross and the Government patrol duty officer are at the same entrance.
- Actors: government
- Ecological mechanism: The raiders layer the curtains over the dust and set the sacks at the entrance between the layers. While the curtains are layered and the filter crates are at the entrance, the positive-pressure doors stay shut toward the outside.
- Choices: The White Cross night-wage duty officer layers the curtains from the inner side of the raiders'' hands and keeps the entrance on the duty side. / The patrol duty officer moves the relay crates that have no dust on them to the side-chamber shelf. / Write the entrance board toward the shut door and leave the raiders'' sacks at the duct mouth.
- Outcomes: If the curtains are layered from the inside, the entrance is on the duty side and the elite squad stands at the outer layer. If the crates are moved, the dust-marked sacks are at the entrance and the protection crates are on the shelf. If the board is written toward the shut door, the band gathers at the duct mouth and only the patrol duty officer is left beside the entrance board.
- Moral cost: The sacks of the elite squad standing at the outer layer hold the night-wage work lamps, and after the band gathers toward the duct the passage of the sealed side chamber runs on into the raiders'' foothold.
- Source dossier: G08

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0274"></a>

## G09 · Sangam Deserters

The Sangam Deserters are soldiers who walked out of the Government army with rifles, ammunition and supplies. The moment they leave the Government they lose their rank and whatever assets they held, and become ordinary citizens with no station to return to. The band has no one above squad leader and no officers, so it cannot lay combined fire with heavy weapons.

- Dossier: [G09 · Sangam Deserters](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G09/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0276"></a>

### G09-SC1 · Removal in the Cold Corridor

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a midwinter night in the cold corridor, fuel drums and ammunition boxes with the Government mark are at the feet of the deserters. The Government inventory watch officer stands beside the work lamp at the end of the corridor, and a contact from the Free Cities has come to the doorway.
- Actors: free-city, government
- Ecological mechanism: The squad leader stands before the inventory ledger page and the elite squad stands between the shelves and the squad leader''s feet. The number of fuel drums is written in the inventory watch officer''s removal column. The contact asks whether any officer is among them.
- Choices: The inventory watch officer bars the removal passage and writes the removal quantity in the ledger. / Hand a few riflemen to the contact in return for fuel drums. / Turn off the work lamp and stand inside the cold warehouse.
- Outcomes: If the passage is barred and the quantity written, the squad leader stands before the ledger page and the elite squad stands beside the shelves. If riflemen are handed over, those people become lowest-class hands on the contact''s side and the fuel drums are at the squad leader''s feet. If the lamp is turned off and the officer stands in the warehouse, the inventory watch officer is inside and the squad leader and elite squad stand beside the fuel drums.
- Moral cost: The fuel drums and ammunition boxes stay at the squad leader''s feet, written in the removal column. The riflemen handed over gain no standing and become hands on another''s ground. The answer that there is no officer is not written as a price for the contact.
- Source dossier: G09

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0278"></a>

### G09-SC2 · The Equipment Pile at the Inspection Window

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: At dawn around the thaw an equipment pile is on the floor of the inspection window and the heat needle sits in a high mark. The Government inspection watch officer stands in the doorway and the inventory watch officer holds the ledger.
- Actors: free-city, government
- Ecological mechanism: The elite squad stands on both sides of the pile and the squad leader stands between the pile and the door of the cold warehouse. The Government mark remains on the fuel drums and tools in the pile. The inspection and inventory watch officers stand in the same doorway.
- Choices: The inspection watch officer locks the door and stands the inspection staff inside the cold warehouse. / The inventory watch officer writes the quantity of the pile in the removal column and sets the ledger before the pile. / Give up the inspection watch officer to be held and take some fuel drums as ransom.
- Outcomes: If the door is locked, the inspection staff are inside the warehouse and the deserters stand beside the pile. If the ledger is set down, the ledger page lies before the pile and the squad leader stands in front of it. If the officer is given up, the officer stands by the elite squad''s shelf post and the fuel drums are stacked at the door.
- Moral cost: The day-duty staff stand inside the locked door. The fuel drums and tools stay on the window floor, written in the removal column. Until the captured officer is released, that post at the inspection window stands empty.
- Source dossier: G09

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0280"></a>

### G09-SC3 · The Cold Train and the Track-Side Door

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: A Government fuel train stands on the track outside the inspection window with the cold loaded on it. The scouts are lined up at the track-side door. The inspection and inventory watch officers stand in the doorway of the cold warehouse.
- Actors: free-city, government
- Ecological mechanism: The train stands with the cold loaded, and few fuel drums remain on the inspection-window side and the warehouse side. The scouts stand before the track-side door, the squad leader beside the remaining fuel drums, and the elite squad beside the row of mineral boxes.
- Choices: The inspection watch officer locks the track-side door and stands the inspection staff inside the warehouse. / The inventory watch officer writes the count of the remaining fuel drums and mineral boxes on one ledger page. / Seize the train driver and hold the train on the track.
- Outcomes: If the door is locked, the scouts stand outside the locked door. If the count is written on one page, the ledger page lies on the doorway floor and the squad leader stands beside the remaining fuel drums. If the driver is seized, the train stands on the track and the driver is beside the elite squad.
- Moral cost: The track-side door is locked and the inspection staff stand inside the warehouse. The count of mineral boxes and remaining fuel drums is written in the ledger. Whoever seizes the driver draws Government pursuit each time a train stops.
- Source dossier: G09

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0282"></a>

## G10 · Bogukmun group

The Bogukmun group moves between Government ground (the Bukhansan Bogukmun shelter corridor) and Arc ground (the lower ration depots of Garak and Jamsil). It is made of people who crossed from the Government to Arc, lost what they held and their territory, and fell to the lowest class, together with people no station would take in. What it is after is grain sacks and bags of distributed seed.

- Dossier: [G10 · Bogukmun group](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G10/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0284"></a>

### G10-SC1 · Bags at the Corridor Threshold

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On an early-summer night the group leader stands at the threshold of the Bukhansan Bogukmun shelter corridor, and the Government seed store is due to send seed bags out through that door. A ration worker stands before the door holding the issue list.
- Actors: arc, government
- Ecological mechanism: The Government corridor duty staff watch the lock of the iron door and the people on the stairs, and the seed-store duty staff watch the shelf of bags. When the list is handed over, the ration worker remains before the door and the attendants stand beside the bags at the threshold.
- Choices: The corridor duty staff lock the iron door and the seed store keeps the bags on the upper shelf. / The duty staff open the iron door only in the morning and send out only the bags on the list. / Give the ration worker up outside the door and pay bags beyond the list as ransom.
- Outcomes: If the iron door is locked, the bags are on the upper shelf and the leader stands outside the door. If it is opened only in the morning, only the listed bags are outside and the attendants stand beside them. If the worker is given up, the worker stands among the elite squad and bags beyond the list are stacked at the threshold.
- Moral cost: After the morning opening, those waiting beyond the list remain on the stairs and part of the day''s seed bags are tied on the upper floor. After the worker is given up, the shelf places of the seed store stand empty, and the released person does not return to the former post.
- Source dossier: G10

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0286"></a>

### G10-SC2 · The Door of the Lower Ration Depot

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: On a ration-day noon the waiting line before the door of the lower ration depot at Jamsil runs up the stairs. The scouts fill the front of the door and the elite squad stands on both sides of the leaf.
- Actors: arc, government
- Ecological mechanism: The Arc ration duty staff have only a rifle-level guard and cannot hold the door for long. As the group stands at the door, grain sacks inside dwindle.
- Choices: The ration duty staff close the door and call on the Government corridor duty staff for help. / Bring part of the sacks out of the door and share them from the front of the waiting line. / Bargain over the sack count with the group leader and have the elite squad withdraw from the door.
- Outcomes: If the door is closed, the line stands up the stairs and help comes one change of cycle later. If sacks are shared out, they empty at the front of the line and the elite squad remains beside the door. If bargained, the elite squad withdraws and the promised sacks are stacked before the door.
- Moral cost: The day''s ration sacks dwindle and the waiting line runs up to the upper stairs. One team of the Government corridor duty staff spends the night on the stair outside the poorly drained stretch. People outside the vent stand in the rains without raincoats.
- Source dossier: G10

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0288"></a>

### G10-SC3 · The Outer Stair the Sea Wind Reaches

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: While the sea wind comes into the corridor, the number on the outer stair grows and the attendants remain inside the corridor with firewood bundles. The group leader is at the threshold.
- Actors: arc, government
- Ecological mechanism: The scouts stand facing the leader. The ration worker stands before the door until the seed store hands over the issue list.
- Choices: The corridor duty staff leave only the lamp of the outer stair lit and lock the inner iron door. / The seed store keeps seed bags on a different shelf from the ration sacks and stops lower-level issue. / The ration worker opens the morning door only after receiving the list and brings out the listed bags.
- Outcomes: If only the outer lamp is lit, the scouts stand on the bright stair and the attendants at the inner threshold are on the dark side. If the bags are moved to another shelf, the seed bags are tied on the upper floor and the lower-floor sacks are before the scouts. If the door is opened after the list is received, only the listed bags are outside and the leader looks at the bags from the threshold.
- Moral cost: On the dimmed stair the people waiting stand in the dark. When seed issue stops, the distributed bags due to Arc and the Free Cities are late. When bags go out only by the list, people not on the list are left outside.
- Source dossier: G10

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0290"></a>

## G11 · Gangnam Stray Humanoids

The Gangnam Stray Humanoids are believers who left Arc ground (Gangnam). Baptism replaced much of their bodies with machinery, but the machine bodies Arc fits take only simple commands. Moving by thought, the direct neural link, must be done by a Free City doctor, and this band has no such doctor.

- Dossier: [G11 · Gangnam Stray Humanoids](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G11/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0292"></a>

### G11-SC1 · Battery Bays at the Convoy Window

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: The battery exchange cycle comes and the Stray Humanoids stand on the footing of the Suseo night convoy window. The Arc corridor duty staff are inside the window. The Arc maintenance ledger is open on the stand.
- Actors: arc
- Ecological mechanism: While the battery count on the window ledge and the bay count of the maintenance ledger agree, exchange proceeds at the ledge. The lead counts the bays before its eyes from the footing. The Arc duty staff hold the window latch.
- Choices: The Arc duty staff lock the convoy window latch and write the battery count in front of the window in the maintenance ledger. / Open exchange up to the bay count of the outlet row. / Write the corridor-side battery movement in the ledger as a slot within the duty time.
- Outcomes: If the latch is locked, the batteries stay inside the window and the lead stays on the footing counting bays. If exchange is opened to the bay count, that quantity of batteries goes to the middle of the row and the ledger''s remaining bays shrink. If the corridor movement is written as duty time, the exchange ends within that time and the departing convoy staff are set after it.
- Moral cost: The night convoy leaves the window later by the time the exchange took. The ledger keeps the duty time of the remaining clinic corridor as a slot of battery movement.
- Source dossier: G11

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0294"></a>

### G11-SC2 · The Division in Front of the Window

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: The Arc corridor duty staff stand at the convoy window with the supply count of the remaining clinic corridor. The maintenance ledger and the window latch lie beside the same stand. The guard stands on the window footing.
- Actors: arc
- Ecological mechanism: Battery bays and corridor time are written together in neighboring columns of one ledger. The guard stands in the row the lead watches, in the direction of the lead''s hand. The guard divides by the bay count of that row.
- Choices: Arc writes the battery count and the corridor time together in one ledger in front of the window. / The Arc corridor staff take the mouth of the remaining clinic corridor and the maintenance staff take the bays of the outlet row. / Put off the departure from the convoy window to a time when the exchange bays are empty.
- Outcomes: If written together, the battery count and the corridor time sit in neighboring columns of one ledger. If the corridor and the outlet row are divided, the corridor staff stand at the mouth and the maintenance staff at the end of the row. If departure is put off, the night convoy staff stand at the window after the exchange bays are emptied.
- Moral cost: Arc passes the corridor supplies to the exchange bays and writes the quantity in the neighboring column of the ledger. The night departure stays at the window for one exchange cycle.
- Source dossier: G11

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0296"></a>

### G11-SC3 · The Repair Request and the Wear Limit

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: A maintenance technician of the Government or the Free Cities arrives at the convoy window. The scouts stand on the footing and hold up the cloth scrap asking for repair. The Arc duty staff are inside the window.
- Actors: arc
- Ecological mechanism: The scouts slow their step on the footing in line with the waiting number. The wear marks on the knee-joint covers go up into the consumption column of the maintenance ledger. The Stray Humanoids move on the maintenance siding within the wear limit of their parts.
- Choices: Let the technician into the window and keep the repair order by waiting number. / Seize the technician and have the repair done at the outlet bay before the lead. / Divide the battery stock between the outlet row and the mouth of the remaining clinic corridor.
- Outcomes: If the technician is let in, the technician works in order beside the Arc duty staff and the scouts stand on the footing. If seized, the technician works at the outlet bay and the number of repaired bays goes up in the ledger. If stock is divided, the battery bays become two piles.
- Moral cost: The faction of the seized technician cuts the maintenance staff who go to and from the window. The consumption column of the ledger keeps the joint covers that reached the wear limit. The duty staff spend the time of the remaining clinic corridor at the mouth on the side of the divided stock.
- Source dossier: G11

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0298"></a>

## G12 · Sinnae Deserters

The Sinnae Deserters are soldiers who walked out of the Government army with rifles, ammunition and supplies. The moment they leave the Government they lose their rank and whatever assets they held, and become ordinary citizens that no station will take in. The band has no one above squad leader and no officers, so it cannot lay combined fire with heavy weapons.

- Dossier: [G12 · Sinnae Deserters](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G12/)

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0300"></a>

### G12-SC1 · Dust-Passage Landing

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a midsummer night the Sinnae Deserters stand in the dust passage of the Sinnae freight elevator, with ammunition boxes. The lift panel is lit. The freight list is on the landing table.
- Actors: government, free-city
- Ecological mechanism: The freight car, the lift panel and the freight list are on one landing. The squad leader is at the radio. The elite squad is at the rail. The freight duty officer is at the threshold.
- Choices: The freight duty officer stacks the freight cages at the car entrance. / The machine duty officer lowers the lift panel breaker. / The freight duty officer hands over some ration tins as ransom and takes back one duty officer.
- Outcomes: If the cages are stacked at the car entrance, the deserters are at the rail and the table. If the breaker is down, the elevator car is on that floor and the machine duty officer is before the panel. If the tins are handed over, the duty officer comes back to the car entrance and a line is drawn through the squad leader''s list.
- Moral cost: The freight duty officer stays inside the cages. That night''s freight car is at the landing. The machine duty officer stands before the breaker on the same floor as the machine gun at the rail. The next day''s rations in the car are fewer by the tins handed over.
- Source dossier: G12

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0302"></a>

### G12-SC2 · The Hatch at the Fan-Inspection Hour

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: At dawn in early autumn the exhaust fan runs in the Yeouido ventilation gallery. The scouts are before the hatch. The Sinnae freight elevator car is below, in the dust passage. A power line runs from the lift panel to that car.
- Actors: government, free-city
- Ecological mechanism: The fan-inspection cycle and the gallery running schedule overlap on the same dawn. The ventilation duty officer is before the damper. The freight duty officer is beside the car. The machine duty officer is before the panel.
- Choices: The ventilation duty officer closes the exhaust damper at the hour of the schedule. / The freight duty officer fixes the freight car at the dust passage floor. / The machine duty officer disconnects the gallery auxiliary power from the lift panel.
- Outcomes: If the damper is closed, the scouts before the hatch and the ventilation duty officer stand in the same warm draught. If the car is fixed at the dust passage floor, the squad leader is at that landing table. If the auxiliary power line lies outside the panel, the hatch lock is in the ventilation duty officer''s hand.
- Moral cost: The warm exhaust pools before the damper. The ventilation duty officer''s fuel tins are in the scouts'' line beside the hatch. The ration tins of the freight car are at the landing.
- Source dossier: G12

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0304"></a>

### G12-SC3 · The Outer Side of the Dock Dust

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: Dock dust is piled on the floor of the outer passage of the Sinnae freight elevator. The scouts are at the outer entrance. The supply, freight and machine duty officers are before the same hour table.
- Actors: government, free-city
- Ecological mechanism: While the dust is in the outer passage the squad leader is at the radio. The elite squad is at the rail. The scouts are at the entrance. The dock duty officer''s dust hour is written in the lower column of the landing table.
- Choices: The dock duty officer closes the dock entry door. / The ventilation duty officer locks the Yeouido ventilation gallery hatch. / The freight and machine duty officers place the cages and the breaker handle in the fully sealed clean section.
- Outcomes: If the dock entry door is closed, the dust is in the passage inside the door and the scouts are before the door. If the gallery hatch is locked, the scouts are divided between the front of the hatch and the outer entrance and the ventilation duty officer is before the valve. If the cages and breaker are in the clean section, the Sinnae Deserters are at the dust passage landing.
- Moral cost: The dock''s freight sacks are inside the entry door. The gallery''s warm exhaust stays before the damper. The two duty officers in the clean section are inside the cages and the dust in the outer passage lies boot-high.
- Source dossier: G12

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0306"></a>

## G13 · Pincer Arm

Night-logistics equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats its last work sheet. It remains in the night sorting hall and dormitory corridor at Guro and Gasan in the Free City districts of Guro-gu and Geumcheon-gu.

It reads light and heat above ground as unclassified stock and approaches. Captured, it becomes workers and a moving barrier that the Arc wakes.

- Dossier: [G13 · Pincer Arm](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G13/)
- Type and use: M5 logistics robot crew, A-U07 woken logistics robots, woken by the Arc

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0389"></a>

## G14 · Empty Cart

Unmanned transport equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats its hour-table tokens. It remains in the Achasan and Guui transfer slots on the Seongbuk Line and the Konkuk corridor in Gwangjin-gu, Government ground.

It gathers around the heat of charging pads and a lit hour lamp. Low in danger, anyone can catch it and use it as a cargo service robot.

- Dossier: [G14 · Empty Cart](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G14/)
- Type and use: M1 service robot, R-U01 cargo service robot, which anyone can catch

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0483"></a>

## G15 · Care Wheel

Care equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats a protection roster that was never updated. Patrol carts run between Myeongdong in Jung-gu and Ttukseom in Seongdong-gu on Government ground and Garak in Songpa-gu on Arc ground.

It reads lit bed lamps and human body heat as people to protect. It does not become a unit; its remaining records are read with Arc software.

- Dossier: [G15 · Care Wheel](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G15/)
- Type and use: M4 care robot, Does not become a unit; its remaining records are read with Arc software

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0567"></a>

## G16 · Girder Spider

Construction equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats unfinished drawings. It remains in the utility tunnels beneath the Suseo drawing vault in Gangnam-gu and on the equipment decks at Amsa and Godeok in Gangdong-gu, Arc ground.

It reads site lights and the heat of distribution boards as unbuilt members and lists them for demolition. Captured, it becomes a trained quadruped unit or a neural-direct quadruped troop.

- Dossier: [G16 · Girder Spider](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G16/)
- Type and use: M2 inspection robot (four-legged), R-U02 stabilized quadruped, F-U15 neural-direct quadruped troop

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0652"></a>

## G17 · One-Eye Tower

Perimeter-watch equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats its last alarm rules. It remains on the Yeouido waterfront masts in Yeongdeungpo-gu (Free City) and the Seoul Station rooftop antennas in Yongsan-gu (Government).

It reads a lamp lit at night and the heat of a warmed sickbed as intrusion and raises the red flag. When the Arc checks the signal and switches it back on, it becomes a woken guard post.

- Dossier: [G17 · One-Eye Tower](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G17/)
- Type and use: M6 blockade watch net, A-U08 woken sentry post, switched back on by the Arc

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0737"></a>

## G18 · Fire-Leg Train

Rail-maintenance equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats its last maintenance orders. It moves between the southwest depot exit lines on the Yeongdeungpo Line (Free City) and the retired sidings at Wangsimni and Yongsan (Government).

It reads the heat of welding arcs and heated rail as defect signals. It does not become a unit; captured, it becomes parts for maintaining rail units.

- Dossier: [G18 · Fire-Leg Train](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G18/)
- Type and use: M3 track and conduit inspection robot, Does not become a unit; it yields parts to maintain rail units

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0859"></a>

## G25 · Lantern Ant

Search-and-rescue swarm drones from before the Great Blackout that repeat their last drill sheet as a real order. They remain in the transmission basement gallery in Mapo-gu and the Yongsan Line research-block test track in Yongsan-gu, Government ground.

They read lit lamps and human body heat as rescue targets and approach in files. Captured, they become search-light drone swarms.

- Dossier: [G25 · Lantern Ant](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G25/)
- Type and use: M12 Lantern Ant, R-U04 searchlight insect-drones

<a id="Hostile-Ecology-Index-heading-0931"></a>

## G26 · Iron Dinosaur

Construction and towing heavy equipment from before the Great Blackout that repeats its last work order. It slept complete at the Changdong depot siding on the Gangnam Line in Dobong-gu (Government) and the Godeok depot on the Gwangjin Line in Gangdong-gu (Arc).

It reads lit lamps and the heat of a warmed maintenance hall as anomalies in the work zone and sounds its alarm. Captured, it becomes hydraulic siege heavy equipment.

- Dossier: [G26 · Iron Dinosaur](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G26/)
- Type and use: M13 Iron Dinosaur, R-U05 hydraulic siege machine
