INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, title_ko, category_id, sort, body_md, body_ko_md) VALUES ('Hostile-Group-G01', 'G01 · Wild Boar', 'G01 · 멧돼지', 'bestiary', 1, '<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0001"></a>

[Ecology bestiary](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0003"></a>

## Boars in the Winter

Wild boars are ordinary wild animals that live on the slopes of Bukhansan to the north, on the foothills of Gwanaksan and Umyeonsan to the south, and in the reed beds of Nanji on the Han River. Since the Great Blackout people have withdrawn from the surface, and the line between mountain and riverbank has faded. As the winter drags on there is nothing to eat in the hills, so boars come down toward the stations where people live. There are no mutated individuals and no leader that commands the herd.

The center of a herd is an older female (sow) who walks with her young. The sow decides when to move, and the younger animals go ahead to check the feeding ground and the way out. Adult males travel alone, use the same feeding grounds, and do not follow the herd. This is the same as real wild boar ecology.

The danger comes in three forms: digging into food stores and food-waste collection yards, meeting people on empty night roads and outdoor stairs outside the stations, and diseases and ticks carried by boars passing to people and livestock.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0006"></a>

## Ecology

| Item | Record |
|---|---|
| Winter movement | When snow piles up, the acorns and roots in the hills are buried. Herds from the slopes of Bukhansan come down along Jeongneung, Pyeongchang-dong, and the Bulgwangcheon stream, herds from Gwanaksan come down along the road toward Namtaeryeong, and the herd in the Nanji reed beds enters the surface settlements of Mapo along the riverbank. |
| Food | They eat acorns, roots, earthworms, insects, and the carcasses of small animals. In winter they also seek out food scraps and grain crumbs that people have put out, and the salt in heaps of calcium chloride stored for de-icing. |
| Breeding and life cycle | Females give birth in spring, but in winters of scarce food breeding falls and the herds range over long distances. More than half of the young do not survive their first winter. A family group splits only when its young survive two seasons in a row. |
| Senses | Smell is sharpest. They tell food, people, and disinfectant apart from far away. Eyesight is weak, and they are sensitive to footsteps and vehicle vibration carried through the ground. They cannot read signals from the machines people use. |
| Herd behavior | The sow leads her young and the younger animals, and the half-grown animals scout ahead. If the sow dies or disappears, the family group circles the same place for a few days and then scatters. Males move alone. |
| Disease and danger | Boars spread African swine fever. It does not pass to people, but a place that raises pigs can lose all of them at once. Eating the raw meat brings parasites and brucellosis, and ticks pass fevers to people. Wounds from tusks and charges run deep, and on the side with no doctors they become serious injuries. |
| Response principles | Shooting scatters the herd into every alley and makes injuries to people more likely. Seal the food firmly, steer the food trail to one place, and split the hours when people move at night. Results are judged not by how many were killed but by how many times they reached a store and how many people were hurt. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0008"></a>

## Herds and Individuals

Below are the boars that come down close to people in winter, set out as herds and individuals.

| Entry | Type | Size | Winter risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| [G01E01 · Sow herd](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e01) | Herd | One sow and four to eight young | Digging into food stores |
| [G01E02 · Subadult scouts](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e02) | Young group | Two to five animals aged one to two | Blocked roads at night |
| [G01E12 · Young](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e12) | Juveniles | Under one year old | Individuals dying of cold |
| [G01E13 · Old adult](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e13) | Individual | Close to ten years old | Starving charges |
| [G01E15 · Lone male](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e15) | Individual | One full-grown male | Collisions in narrow passages |
| [G01E16 · Bukhansan slope population](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e16) | Regional population | Several dozen animals | Threat to ridge trails and hillside villages |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0011"></a>
<a id="g01e01"></a>

### G01E01 · Sow herd

- Size and place: They come down from the slopes of Bukhansan, the foothills of Gwanaksan, and the Nanji reed beds.
- Winter behavior: The sow decides when to move, and the young follow. When snow falls they circle food-waste yards and grain stores.
- Danger: They dig under store walls and tear open grain sacks. If the young are touched, the sow charges at once.
- Response: Seal the food and never stand between the sow and her young. If only the sow is taken, the young keep returning to the same place for days.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0034"></a>
<a id="g01e02"></a>

### G01E02 · Subadult scouts

- Size and place: They check the feeding grounds and escape routes ahead of the family group.
- Winter behavior: At night they sniff around station exits and outdoor stairs, then return and bring the family group.
- Danger: If they meet a person in the dark they panic and ram.
- Response: Keep exit lights on and leave no food smell under the stairs. If the scouts are driven off, the main herd comes by another route.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0264"></a>
<a id="g01e12"></a>

### G01E12 · Young

- Size and place: They stay beside the sow and sleep in brush and hollows.
- Winter behavior: Many die in the first month of winter. Those that survive quickly grow used to the food people put out.
- Danger: Animals that have grown used to coming near people become bolder as they grow.
- Response: Do not feed the young. If they are fed, the same animals come closer the next winter.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0287"></a>
<a id="g01e13"></a>

### G01E13 · Old adult

- Size and place: It lives apart at the edge of the herd.
- Winter behavior: Its teeth are worn and it cannot eat hard roots, so it comes close to the stations looking for food people have thrown away.
- Danger: A starving, wounded animal sometimes stands its ground in front of people instead of running.
- Response: Do not approach, and leave a path open. Watch from a distance, and shut the gate after it passes.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0333"></a>
<a id="g01e15"></a>

### G01E15 · Lone male

- Size and place: It lives alone, apart from the family group.
- Winter behavior: It uses the same feeding grounds but does not follow the sow’s movements. If it meets a person or a cart on the road it does not give way.
- Danger: Its tusks are long and it is strong, so ramming a cart or a person causes severe injury.
- Response: Do not walk alone at night. Warn it off at passages with light and noise.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0356"></a>
<a id="g01e16"></a>

### G01E16 · Bukhansan slope population

- Size and place: It lives on the slopes of Bukhansan and in the surface villages below the ridge. The land is Government land in Seongbuk, Gangbuk, and Eunpyeong.
- Winter behavior: When snow falls it comes down from the ridge and passes in front of the surface houses of Jeongneung and Pyeongchang-dong. When the snow melts it climbs back up.
- Danger: At night it digs up surface houses and vegetable plots and blocks people on the forest paths. Surface villagers meet it first.
- Response: Open the ridge path only by day, and fence the vegetable plots to change the food trail.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0379"></a>

## Habitat and Winter Response

A boar’s territory is set not by lines on a map but by the depth of snow and the smell of food. In the north they come down from the slopes of Bukhansan along Jeongneung, Pyeongchang-dong, and the Bulgwangcheon stream. In the west, from Nanji on the Han River, they follow reed beds and the riverbank levee toward the surface villages of Mapo. In the south they come down from the foothills of Gwanaksan and Umyeonsan along the Namtaeryeong road toward Gwanak and Seocho.

On Government land in the north (Seongbuk, Gangbuk, Eunpyeong, Mapo), surface villages and vegetable plots suffer first. The Government has no doctors, so the White Cross can give only first aid, and anyone badly torn by tusks does not come back. On the Free City side in Gwanak there are doctors who can treat wounds, but people carry the cost of feeding. On Arc land in Seocho the Umyeonsan herd comes around station entrances and grain stores.

Boars enter the stations by three routes: open entrances, gaps where a shutter does not fully close, and vents. The deep underground levels can be sealed with shutters and are safe, but people who live on the ground floor and above must hold out behind a single wall or fence. This is why the higher people live, the greater the danger.

The best response is to change the food trail. Close the food-waste yards and grain stores tightly, and open a detour with food placed deliberately where nobody is, to lead the herd that way. Shooting scatters the family group into the alleys and hurts more people. Young that have lost their sow search the same place over and over for days, so night patrols should rest in the meantime.

Results are not measured by the number killed. Look at how many times they reached a store, how long a road was blocked, how many people were hurt, and how much vegetable plot was dug up. If intrusions fall but traces of the young vanish too, that is not stability but a sign that hunger and capture pressure have grown.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0442"></a>

## Linked scenarios

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0443"></a>

### G01-SC1 · The First Hoof Outside the Embankment

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: The day after the first snow settles, the prints of a sow and her young appear in front of a grain store outside the embankment on the Nanji riverbank of the Han River.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Mapo, White Cross, surface villagers
- Ecological mechanism: When food is buried under the snow, the sow leads the family group to the places where people put out food waste.
- Choices: Delay the collection yard’s night operation / Open a reed-bed detour with a scent lure / Capture only the sow
- Outcomes: Delaying operations delays the ration too. A detour creates a long-term trail. Taking the sow leaves the young circling the same place for days.
- Moral cost: The people’s ration and the survival of a family group with young collide on the same timetable.
- Source dossier: G01

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0445"></a>

### G01-SC2 · The Price of the Salt Pool

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: When heaps of calcium chloride for de-icing are piled in front of a station, the herd moves its feeding ground to the semi-basement alleys and surface villages in search of salt.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the Government and the Free Cities, ordinary citizens, road-clearing workers
- Ecological mechanism: The sow that knows where the salt is leads the family group back to the same spot. Even if the heap is removed the herd does not vanish; it is pushed into a more dangerous village.
- Choices: Leave a deliberately placed salt pool in one spot / Prioritize recovering alley scraps and salt / Lower the density with a hunting permit
- Outcomes: The pool fixes the cost of coexistence. Prioritizing recovery takes labor away from other rations. Hunting makes the remaining animals better at avoiding people.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to keep the danger in a safe place and manage it, or to pass it on to a vulnerable alley.
- Source dossier: G01

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0447"></a>

### G01-SC3 · Right of Passage on the Flood Line

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: A column of porters from outside Seoul passes along the flood line on the Han River levee, and the Bukhansan herd comes down the same road.
- Actors: Government stationmaster, porters and refugees from outside Seoul, White Cross
- Ecological mechanism: In the snow the only dry ground is the road on the levee, so people and boars use the same road. Food crumbs dropped by the porters draw the herd in.
- Choices: Divide the passage hours / Turn the food convoy onto a bait trail toward the reed beds / Close the levee road for a while
- Outcomes: Dividing the hours reduces collisions, but people at the back endure the cold longer. The bait trail uses convoy crews and containers. Closing the road drives the porters to other stations.
- Moral cost: The fastest passage and the safest passage are hard to achieve together.
- Source dossier: G01
', '<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0001"></a>

[생태 도감](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0003"></a>

## 겨울의 멧돼지

멧돼지는 서울 북쪽 북한산 기슭과 남쪽 관악산·우면산 자락, 한강 난지 쪽 갈대밭에서 사는 보통의 야생 멧돼지다. 대정전 뒤 사람이 땅 위에서 물러나면서 산과 강변의 경계가 사라졌고, 겨울이 길어지자 산에는 먹을 것이 없어 멧돼지가 사람이 사는 역 가까이로 내려온다. 변이한 개체도, 무리를 지휘하는 우두머리도 없다.

무리의 중심은 새끼를 데리고 다니는 나이 든 암컷(모돈)이다. 모돈이 이동을 정하고, 덜 자란 개체가 앞서 먹이터와 퇴로를 살핀다. 다 큰 수컷은 혼자 다니며 같은 먹이터를 쓰되 무리를 따르지 않는다. 이것은 현실의 멧돼지 생태와 같다.

위험은 세 가지다. 식량 창고와 음식물 집하장을 파헤치는 것, 사람이 드문 밤길과 역 바깥 계단에서 사람과 마주치는 것, 그리고 멧돼지에 붙은 병과 진드기가 사람과 가축에게 옮는 것이다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0006"></a>

## 생태 정보

| 항목 | 기록 |
|---|---|
| 겨울 이동 | 눈이 쌓이면 산 위의 도토리와 뿌리가 눈 밑에 묻힌다. 북한산 기슭의 무리는 정릉·평창동·불광천을 따라, 관악산 무리는 남태령 쪽 도로를 따라 내려온다. 한강 난지 갈대밭 무리는 강변을 따라 마포 쪽 지상 마을로 들어온다. |
| 먹이 | 도토리, 뿌리, 지렁이, 곤충, 작은 동물의 사체를 먹는다. 겨울에는 사람이 내놓은 음식물과 곡물 부스러기, 제설용으로 쌓아 둔 염화칼슘 더미의 소금기도 찾는다. |
| 번식과 생애 | 암컷은 봄에 새끼를 낳지만 먹이가 모자란 겨울에는 번식이 줄고 먼 거리를 옮겨 다닌다. 새끼의 절반 이상이 첫 겨울을 넘기지 못한다. 가족군은 새끼가 두 계절 연속 살아남을 때만 갈라진다. |
| 감각 | 후각이 가장 날카롭다. 음식물, 사람, 소독약 냄새를 멀리서 가려낸다. 시력은 약하고, 땅을 통해 오는 발소리와 차량 진동에 민감하다. 사람이 쓰는 기계의 신호는 읽지 못한다. |
| 무리 행동 | 모돈이 새끼와 어린 개체를 이끌고, 덜 자란 개체가 앞서 살핀다. 모돈이 죽거나 사라지면 가족군은 며칠 동안 같은 자리를 맴돌다가 흩어진다. 수컷은 혼자 움직인다. |
| 병과 위험 | 멧돼지는 아프리카돼지열병을 옮기는데, 사람에게는 옮지 않지만 돼지를 기르는 곳은 한 번에 잃는다. 날고기를 먹으면 기생충과 브루셀라균에 걸리고, 진드기는 사람에게 열병을 옮긴다. 엄니와 돌진에 다치면 상처가 깊어, 의사가 없는 쪽에서는 중상이 된다. |
| 대응 원칙 | 총으로 잡으면 무리가 골목마다 흩어져 사람이 다치기 쉽다. 먹이를 단단히 막고, 먹이 길을 한 곳으로 돌리고, 밤에 사람이 다니는 시간을 나눈다. 성과는 죽인 수가 아니라 창고에 들어온 횟수와 사람이 다친 횟수로 본다. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0008"></a>

## 무리와 개체

아래는 겨울에 사람 가까이로 내려오는 멧돼지를 무리와 개체로 나누어 적은 것이다.

| 항목 | 구분 | 규모 | 겨울 위험 |
|---|---|---|---|
| [G01E01 · 모돈 가족군](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e01) | 무리 | 모돈 1마리와 새끼 4~8마리 | 식량 창고 파헤침 |
| [G01E02 · 아성체 정찰조](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e02) | 어린 무리 | 1~2살 개체 2~5마리 | 야간 길 막힘 |
| [G01E12 · 새끼](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e12) | 어린 개체 | 생후 1년 이하 | 추위로 죽는 개체 |
| [G01E13 · 늙은 성체](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e13) | 개체 | 10살 가까운 개체 | 굶주린 돌진 |
| [G01E15 · 단독 수컷](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e15) | 개체 | 다 자란 수컷 1마리 | 길목 충돌 |
| [G01E16 · 북한산 기슭 개체군](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G01/#g01e16) | 지역 개체군 | 수십 마리 | 등산로와 능선 마을 위협 |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0011"></a>
<a id="g01e01"></a>

### G01E01 · 모돈 가족군

- 규모·곳: 북한산 기슭과 관악산 자락, 난지 갈대밭에서 내려온다.
- 겨울 행동: 모돈이 이동을 정하고 새끼가 뒤를 따른다. 눈이 올 때 음식물 집하장과 곡물 창고 둘레를 돈다.
- 위험: 창고 벽 밑을 파고 곡물 자루를 찢는다. 새끼를 건드리면 모돈이 곧바로 돌진한다.
- 대응: 먹이를 막고 모돈과 새끼 사이에 서지 않는다. 모돈만 잡으면 새끼가 며칠 동안 같은 자리를 되풀이해 찾는다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0034"></a>
<a id="g01e02"></a>

### G01E02 · 아성체 정찰조

- 규모·곳: 가족군 앞에서 먹이터와 퇴로를 살핀다.
- 겨울 행동: 밤에 역 출구와 바깥 계단 근처 냄새를 맡고 돌아가 가족군을 데려온다.
- 위험: 어둠 속에서 사람과 마주치면 놀라 들이받는다.
- 대응: 출구 불을 끄지 않고, 계단 밑에 음식 냄새가 남지 않게 한다. 정찰조를 쫓으면 본대가 다른 길로 온다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0264"></a>
<a id="g01e12"></a>

### G01E12 · 새끼

- 규모·곳: 모돈 곁에서 지내며 덤불과 구덩이에서 잔다.
- 겨울 행동: 겨울 첫 달에 많이 죽는다. 살아남은 새끼는 사람이 내놓은 음식에 빨리 익숙해진다.
- 위험: 사람 곁에 오는 데 익숙해진 개체는 크면 더 대담해진다.
- 대응: 새끼에게 음식을 주지 않는다. 먹이를 주면 다음 겨울에 같은 개체가 더 가까이 온다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0287"></a>
<a id="g01e13"></a>

### G01E13 · 늙은 성체

- 규모·곳: 무리 가장자리에서 따로 지낸다.
- 겨울 행동: 이가 닳아 단단한 뿌리를 못 먹고, 사람이 버린 음식을 찾아 역 가까이 온다.
- 위험: 굶주리고 다친 몸으로 사람 앞에서 도망치지 않고 버티는 일이 있다.
- 대응: 다가가지 말고 길을 열어 준다. 멀리서 지켜보다 지나가면 문을 닫는다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0333"></a>
<a id="g01e15"></a>

### G01E15 · 단독 수컷

- 규모·곳: 가족군에서 떨어져 혼자 산다.
- 겨울 행동: 같은 먹이터를 쓰지만 모돈의 이동에 따르지 않는다. 길을 가다가 사람이나 수레를 만나면 비키지 않는다.
- 위험: 엄니가 길고 힘이 세서 수레와 사람을 들이받으면 크게 다친다.
- 대응: 밤에 혼자 걷지 않는다. 길목에 불과 소리로 알려 놓는다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0356"></a>
<a id="g01e16"></a>

### G01E16 · 북한산 기슭 개체군

- 규모·곳: 북한산 기슭과 능선 아래 지상 마을에 산다. 성북·강북·은평 쪽 정부 땅이다.
- 겨울 행동: 눈이 오면 능선에서 내려와 정릉·평창동 쪽 지상 집들 앞을 지난다. 눈이 녹으면 다시 올라간다.
- 위험: 밤에 지상 집과 텃밭을 파헤치고, 숲길을 지나는 사람을 막는다. 지상 마을 사람이 먼저 겪는다.
- 대응: 능선 길을 낮에만 열고, 텃밭 둘레에 울타리를 둘러 먹이 길을 바꾼다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0379"></a>

## 서식지와 겨울 대응

멧돼지의 땅은 지도의 선이 아니라 눈의 깊이와 먹이 냄새가 정한다. 북쪽에서는 북한산 기슭에서 정릉, 평창동, 불광천을 따라 내려온다. 서쪽 한강 난지에서는 갈대밭과 강변 둑을 따라 마포 쪽 지상 마을로 이어진다. 남쪽에서는 관악산과 우면산 자락에서 남태령 도로를 따라 관악·서초 쪽으로 내려온다.

정부의 북쪽 땅(성북·강북·은평·마포)에서는 지상 마을과 텃밭이 먼저 피해를 입는다. 정부에는 의사가 없어 흰십자단이 응급 처치까지만 하고, 엄니에 찢긴 중상자는 돌아오지 못한다. 자유도시 관악 쪽은 의사가 있어 상처를 치료할 수 있지만 먹이는 사람이 부담한다. 아크의 서초 쪽 땅에서는 우면산 무리가 역 출입구와 곡물 창고 둘레로 온다.

멧돼지가 역 안으로 들어오는 길은 세 곳이다. 열린 출입구, 셔터가 완전히 닫히지 않은 틈, 환기구다. 지하 깊은 층은 셔터로 막을 수 있어 안전하지만, 지상 1층부터 위에 사는 사람은 담과 울타리 하나로 버텨야 한다. 사는 높이가 높을수록 위험이 큰 이유다.

좋은 대응은 먹이 길을 바꾸는 것이다. 음식물 집하장과 곡물 창고를 단단히 닫고, 일부러 사람이 없는 곳에 먹이를 둔 우회로를 열어 무리를 그쪽으로 이끈다. 총으로 잡으면 가족군이 골목으로 흩어져 사람이 더 다친다. 모돈을 잃은 새끼들은 며칠 동안 같은 자리를 되풀이해 찾아다니므로, 그동안 밤 순찰은 쉬어야 한다.

성과는 죽인 수로 재지 않는다. 창고에 들어온 횟수, 길이 막혔던 시간, 사람이 다친 횟수, 파헤쳐진 텃밭의 넓이로 본다. 침입이 줄었는데 새끼의 흔적도 같이 사라졌다면 안정이 아니라 굶주림과 포획 압력이 커졌다는 뜻이다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0442"></a>

## 연결 시나리오

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0443"></a>

### G01-SC1 · 제방 밖 첫 발굽

- 단계: 1
- 촉발: 눈이 처음 쌓인 다음 날, 난지 한강변 제방 바깥 곡물 창고 앞에 모돈과 새끼 발자국이 나타난다.
- 관련 세력: 정부 마포 쪽 역장, 흰십자단, 지상 마을 사람
- 생태 기제: 눈 밑에 먹이가 묻히자 모돈이 가족군을 이끌고 사람이 음식물을 내놓는 곳을 찾는다.
- 선택지: 집하장 야간 운영을 늦춘다 / 냄새 미끼로 갈대밭 우회로를 연다 / 모돈만 포획한다
- 결과: 운영을 늦추면 배급도 늦는다. 우회로는 장기 이동 길을 만든다. 모돈을 잡으면 새끼가 며칠 동안 같은 자리를 찾아 돈다.
- 도덕 비용: 사람의 배급과 새끼가 딸린 가족군이 사는 일이 같은 시간표에서 부딪힌다.
- 원본 항목: G01

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0445"></a>

### G01-SC2 · 염분 웅덩이의 값

- 단계: 2
- 촉발: 제설용 염화칼슘 더미를 역 앞에 쌓아 두자, 무리가 소금기를 찾아 반지하 골목과 지상 마을로 먹이터를 옮긴다.
- 관련 세력: 정부와 자유도시의 역장, 일반 시민, 길 닦는 일꾼
- 생태 기제: 소금이 있는 곳을 아는 모돈이 가족군을 이끌고 같은 자리로 돌아온다. 더미를 치워도 무리는 사라지지 않고 더 위험한 마을로 밀려난다.
- 선택지: 일부러 둔 염분 웅덩이를 한 곳에 남긴다 / 골목 음식물과 소금 회수를 먼저 한다 / 수렵 허가로 밀도를 낮춘다
- 결과: 웅덩이를 남기면 공존의 비용이 고정된다. 회수를 앞세우면 다른 배급 노동이 빠진다. 수렵을 하면 남은 개체가 사람을 더 잘 피하게 된다.
- 도덕 비용: 안전한 곳에 위험을 남겨 관리할지, 약한 골목으로 위험을 넘길지 정해야 한다.
- 원본 항목: G01

<a id="Hostile-Group-G01-heading-0447"></a>

### G01-SC3 · 범람선의 통행권

- 단계: 3
- 촉발: 한강 둑 위 범람선을 따라 서울 밖에서 온 짐꾼 행렬이 지나가는데, 같은 길로 북한산 무리가 내려온다.
- 관련 세력: 정부 역장, 서울 밖에서 온 짐꾼과 피난민, 흰십자단
- 생태 기제: 눈 속에서 마른 땅은 둑 위 길뿐이라 사람과 멧돼지가 같은 길을 쓴다. 짐꾼이 흘린 음식 부스러기가 무리를 끌어들인다.
- 선택지: 통행 시간을 나눈다 / 음식 호송을 갈대밭 쪽 미끼 길로 돌린다 / 둑 위 길을 한동안 닫는다
- 결과: 시간을 나누면 부딪힘이 줄지만 뒷줄 사람은 추위를 더 오래 견딘다. 미끼 길은 호송 인원과 용기를 쓴다. 길을 닫으면 짐꾼이 다른 역으로 몰린다.
- 도덕 비용: 가장 빠른 통행과 가장 안전한 통행은 함께 이루기 어렵다.
- 원본 항목: G01
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, title_ko, category_id, sort, body_md, body_ko_md) VALUES ('Hostile-Group-G02', 'G02 · Large-billed Crow', 'G02 · 큰부리까마귀', 'bestiary', 2, '<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0001"></a>

[Ecology bestiary](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0003"></a>

## Large-billed Crows in the Winter

The large-billed crow is the commonest crow in Seoul. It has a heavy bill, a domed forehead, and a sharp mind. In a city thinned out since the Great Blackout, rooftops, pylons, and the steel frames of Han River bridges have become good roosts for crows. In winter, with food short, hundreds gather at a single roost, and by day they fly to station exits, grain stores, and food-waste yards where people put out food. There is no special leader and no individual that commands signals.

Crows imitate and remember sounds well. They learn repeated sounds such as horns, inspection bells, and the start-up hum of transformers as signals of food or danger, and spread them through the flock. This is the same as real crow ecology. They cannot read human radio or communication content.

The danger comes in three forms: tearing open grain sacks and food-waste yards to steal food, pecking at wire insulation and ceramic insulators and soiling them with droppings, causing short circuits and communication outages, and avian influenza and droppings-borne pathogens carried by crows that contaminate water and grain. In the breeding season of spring they dive at people passing near nests, but in winter attacks on people are rare.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0006"></a>

## Ecology

| Item | Record |
|---|---|
| Winter movement | At the end of autumn, scattered families gather at large communal roosts. The factory pylons of Daerim and Garibong, the roofs along the Yeongdeungpo line, the overhead-wire crossings at Wangsimni, and the steel frames of Han River bridges serve as roosts. In the morning they scatter to feeding grounds, and at dusk they return. |
| Food | They eat insects, earthworms, grain, food scraps, and the carcasses of small animals. In winter, food people have thrown away, gaps in grain stores, and market leftovers make up a large share. When nesting they take thin wire stripped of insulation and fabric scraps. |
| Breeding and life cycle | In spring a pair holds a single pylon and builds a nest. In winter they do not breed, and several families stay together at a communal roost. If one of a pair vanishes, a neighboring pair tests the empty spot, and if a nest is torn down by force, young birds set up a new roost nearby. |
| Senses | By sight they read people’s helmets, poles, and flashlight reflections, and by hearing they sort the order of horns and machine sounds. At night their eyes are poor and they hardly fly, but they gather around bright exits and floodlights. They cannot read the content of radio signals. |
| Flock behavior | A communal roost has no commander. When one bird raises an alarm, those around it fly up. Alarm calls and dives are done together, and when one finds food, dozens soon gather. |
| Disease and danger | Crows carry avian influenza, and their droppings contaminate water and grain. When droppings build up on insulators, damp causes short circuits and cuts communication and lighting. Pecked insulation and torn sacks become a way in for rats. |
| Response principles | Close the gaps in grain stores and collection yards, replace wire insulation on time, and clear droppings under roosts. If a sound is changed, false alarms rise for a few days and the birds soon learn the new one, so keep using the same alarm. Chasing them with gunfire only moves the roost to the next tower. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0008"></a>

## Flocks and Individuals

Below are the large-billed crows that come down close to people in winter, set out as flocks and individuals.

| Entry | Type | Size | Winter risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| [G02E01 · Breeding pair](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e01) | Pair | Two adults | Dives near the nest |
| [G02E02 · Communal roost flock](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e02) | Flock | Several hundred birds | Grain store and wire contamination |
| [G02E12 · Fledged young](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e12) | Young birds | Several dozen young birds | Random occupation |
| [G02E13 · Old bird](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e13) | Individual | Over ten years old | Delayed alarm response |
| [G02E10 · Daerim and Garibong pylon flock](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e10) | Regional flock | Several hundred birds | Wire and insulator contamination |
| [G02E11 · Han River bridge roost flock](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e11) | Regional flock | Dozens to hundreds of birds | Obstruction of bridge traffic |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0011"></a>
<a id="g02e01"></a>

### G02E01 · Breeding pair

- Size and place: They nest on pylons and bridge steel frames.
- Winter behavior: In spring they hold one tower and share carrying food. In winter they return to the communal roost.
- Danger: In the breeding season they dive at people passing near the nest. This is rare in winter.
- Response: In spring, go around the passage under the nest. If it is torn down, a new roost appears beside it.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0034"></a>
<a id="g02e02"></a>

### G02E02 · Communal roost flock

- Size and place: Daerim and Garibong pylons, Wangsimni overhead wires, Han River bridge steel frames.
- Winter behavior: By day they scatter to feeding grounds and return at dusk. They learn sounds from one another.
- Danger: They tear grain sacks and peck wire insulation, and droppings build up under the roost.
- Response: Close the store gaps and clean under the roost. If driven off, they move to the next tower.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0264"></a>
<a id="g02e12"></a>

### G02E12 · Fledged young

- Size and place: They leave their parents and try out new roosts on nearby school paths and rooftops.
- Winter behavior: At the edge of the flock they copy the sounds of the adults.
- Danger: Inexperienced, many die in collisions with people and vehicles.
- Response: Clear food off the roads to reduce accidents.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0287"></a>
<a id="g02e13"></a>

### G02E13 · Old bird

- Size and place: A low branch at the edge of the communal roost.
- Winter behavior: It responds only to familiar sounds and is slow to react to unfamiliar alarms.
- Danger: Having followed long-learned sounds, it takes off late for a new alarm.
- Response: When changing an alarm, first play the same sound alongside it for a few days.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0218"></a>
<a id="g02e10"></a>

### G02E10 · Daerim and Garibong pylon flock

- Size and place: Factory pylons in Daerim and Garibong in Yeongdeungpo-gu, and in Guro. This is Free City land.
- Winter behavior: They sleep on the Yeongdeungpo-line roofs and pylons, and by day circle the factory sites and market alleys.
- Danger: Insulators covered with droppings short out and cut the workshops’ electricity in the Free City.
- Response: Regularly clear droppings under the pylons and replace insulation so it does not become nesting material.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0241"></a>
<a id="g02e11"></a>

### G02E11 · Han River bridge roost flock

- Size and place: Steel frames of Han River bridges. Whose land it is depends on the districts the bridge connects.
- Winter behavior: They sleep in inspection openings and steel gaps. Each morning they pick up food dropped by vehicles on the bridge.
- Danger: Droppings on the bridge are slippery, and people and carts fall. Dives sometimes stop traffic.
- Response: Close bridge inspection openings and clear food scraps to make the roost less usable.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0379"></a>

## Habitat and Winter Response

A large-billed crow’s territory is set by its roosts. In the west the factory pylons of Daerim, Garibong, and Guro in Yeongdeungpo-gu (Free City land), and in the east the overhead-wire crossings at Wangsimni in Seongdong-gu (Government land), are the large roosts. The steel frames of Han River bridges belong to whichever faction holds the districts the bridge reaches. As winter deepens they move for a time along the river in search of insects and fish carcasses on the western Han River bank.

The Free City has many technicians who repair workshops and electricity by hand and can replace insulation themselves, but there are too few people to tend every pylon. The Government spends labor on inspecting the overhead wires near Wangsimni, but with no doctors, someone who falls from a pylon gets first aid only. The people working at height carry the greatest risk.

Crows do not slip through shutter gaps, but they gather in front of open exits and vents and target food-waste yards and grain stores. People who live deep underground can seal themselves off with shutters. People who live on the surface must guard the grain sacks under their roofs and eaves themselves.

Good response means blocking the food and changing the roost. Keep grain and food scraps in lidded containers and replace wire insulation on time to remove nesting material. Do not change the sounds; keep using the same alarm. Results are judged not by how many were driven off but by short-circuit counts, grain lost, and time communication was down.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0445"></a>

## Linked scenarios

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0446"></a>

### G02-SC1 · Three Horns

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a winter morning at the overhead-wire crossing in Wangsimni, when a maintenance vehicle sounds its horn three times, the crows at the communal roost make the same sound.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Seongdong, overhead-wire maintenance workers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Crows learn a repeated sound as a signal that food is coming and spread it. When the sound is heard they gather in front of the grain store.
- Choices: Change the horn sound / Use flags instead of sound / Move the grain store
- Outcomes: Changing the sound raises false alarms for a few days, and the birds soon learn the new one. Flags spread less among the birds. Moving the store takes labor.
- Moral cost: Decide whether to reduce first the safety of the workers or the cost of driving the birds off.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0448"></a>

### G02-SC2 · The False Blackout Alarm

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: After crows peck at insulators and droppings pile up, the electricity in the Daerim workshops cuts out and a blackout alarm sounds.
- Actors: Free City stationmaster in Daerim, workshop technicians, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Damp and droppings cause a short circuit and the alarm sounds, and the crows learn that sound as a signal of food.
- Choices: Replace and clean the insulators first / Switch off the alarm sound for a while / Put an off-limits cord around the pylon
- Outcomes: Replacing the insulators restores electricity but takes labor. Switching the alarm off risks missing a real blackout. The off-limits cord does not stop the birds.
- Moral cost: Keeping the workshops powered collides with the safety of the people given the dangerous pylon work.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0450"></a>

### G02-SC3 · A New Signal on the Bridge

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: After a large roost forms in the inspection openings on a Han River bridge, traffic on the bridge stops every dawn.
- Actors: Stationmasters of the districts the bridge reaches, porters and cart-pullers, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: Gaps in the bridge steel become a sleeping place out of snow and wind, so crows gather, and food dropped by vehicles draws the birds each morning.
- Choices: Close the inspection openings / Shift the passage hours / Clear the food off the bridge
- Outcomes: Closing the openings shrinks the roost but adds inspection labor. Shifting the hours leaves porters standing longer in the cold. Clearing the food moves the birds to the next bridge.
- Moral cost: Decide who bears the bridge inspection labor and the burden on the next bridge the birds move to.
- Source dossier: G02
', '<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0001"></a>

[생태 도감](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0003"></a>

## 겨울의 큰부리까마귀

큰부리까마귀는 서울에서 가장 흔한 까마귀다. 부리가 굵고 이마가 불룩하며 영리하다. 대정전 뒤 사람이 줄어든 도시의 옥상과 철탑, 한강 다리의 철골은 까마귀에게 알맞은 잠자리가 되었다. 겨울에는 먹이가 모자라 수백 마리가 한 횃대에 모이고, 낮에는 사람이 먹을 것을 내놓는 역 출구와 곡물 창고, 음식물 집하장으로 날아든다. 특별한 우두머리도, 신호를 지휘하는 개체도 없다.

까마귀는 소리를 잘 흉내 내고 기억한다. 경적, 점검 종, 변압기 기동음처럼 되풀이되는 소리를 먹이나 위험의 신호로 익혀 무리 안에 퍼뜨린다. 이것은 현실의 까마귀 생태와 같다. 사람의 전파나 통신 내용을 읽지는 못한다.

위험은 세 가지다. 곡물 자루와 음식물 집하장을 찢어 먹이를 훔치는 것, 전선 피복과 절연 애자를 쪼고 배설물로 더럽혀 합선과 통신 두절을 일으키는 것, 그리고 까마귀가 옮기는 조류 인플루엔자와 배설물 속 병원균이 물과 곡물을 오염시키는 것이다. 번식기인 봄에는 둥지 가까이 지나가는 사람에게 급강하하지만, 겨울에는 사람을 공격하는 일이 드물다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0006"></a>

## 생태 정보

| 항목 | 기록 |
|---|---|
| 겨울 이동 | 가을 끝에 흩어져 있던 가족이 큰 공동 횃대로 모인다. 대림·가리봉의 공장 철탑과 영등포선 지붕, 왕십리 가선 교차점, 한강 다리의 철골이 횃대다. 아침에 흩어져 먹이터로 날고 해 질 무렵 돌아온다. |
| 먹이 | 곤충, 지렁이, 곡물, 음식물 찌꺼기, 작은 동물의 사체를 먹는다. 겨울에는 사람이 버린 음식과 곡물 창고의 틈, 시장 잔여물이 큰 몫이다. 둥지를 틀 때는 피복이 벗겨진 가는 전선과 섬유 조각을 가져간다. |
| 번식과 생애 | 봄에 한 쌍이 철탑 하나를 지키며 둥지를 튼다. 겨울에는 번식하지 않고 여러 가족이 공동 횃대에서 지낸다. 한쪽이 사라지면 이웃 쌍이 빈 자리를 시험하고, 강제로 둥지를 철거하면 어린 개체가 가까운 곳에 새 횃대를 만든다. |
| 감각 | 시각으로 사람의 헬멧, 막대, 손전등 반사를 읽고 청각으로 경적과 기계음의 순서를 가른다. 밤에는 눈이 어두워 거의 날지 않지만, 환한 출구와 투광등에는 모여든다. 전파의 내용은 읽지 못한다. |
| 무리 행동 | 공동 횃대에는 지휘자가 없다. 한 마리가 위험을 알리면 주변이 따라 날아오른다. 경계 울음과 급강하는 여럿이 함께 하고, 한 곳에서 먹이를 찾으면 곧 수십 마리가 모인다. |
| 병과 위험 | 까마귀는 조류 인플루엔자를 옮기고 배설물이 물과 곡물을 오염시킨다. 배설물이 절연체에 쌓이면 습기로 합선이 나 통신과 조명이 끊긴다. 쪼인 피복과 찢긴 자루는 쥐가 들어오는 길이 된다. |
| 대응 원칙 | 곡물 창고와 집하장 틈을 막고, 전선 피복을 제때 갈고, 횃대 밑 배설물을 치운다. 소리를 바꾸면 며칠 동안 오경보가 늘고 곧 새 소리를 익히므로, 같은 경보음을 꾸준히 쓴다. 총으로 쫓으면 횃대가 옆 탑으로 옮겨 갈 뿐이다. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0008"></a>

## 무리와 개체

아래는 겨울에 사람 가까이로 내려오는 큰부리까마귀를 무리와 개체로 나누어 적은 것이다.

| 항목 | 구분 | 규모 | 겨울 위험 |
|---|---|---|---|
| [G02E01 · 번식쌍](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e01) | 한 쌍 | 성체 2마리 | 둥지 근처 급강하 |
| [G02E02 · 공동 횃대 무리](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e02) | 무리 | 수백 마리 | 곡물 창고와 전선 오염 |
| [G02E12 · 이소한 어린 개체](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e12) | 어린 개체 | 어린 새 수십 마리 | 난잡한 점유 |
| [G02E13 · 늙은 개체](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e13) | 개체 | 10살 넘은 개체 | 경보 지체 |
| [G02E10 · 대림·가리봉 철탑 군집](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e10) | 지역 군집 | 수백 마리 | 전선과 절연체 오염 |
| [G02E11 · 한강 다리 횃대 군집](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G02/#g02e11) | 지역 군집 | 수십~수백 마리 | 다리 위 통행 방해 |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0011"></a>
<a id="g02e01"></a>

### G02E01 · 번식쌍

- 규모·곳: 철탑과 다리 철골에 둥지를 튼다.
- 겨울 행동: 봄에 한 탑을 지키고 먹이 운반을 나눈다. 겨울에는 공동 횃대로 돌아간다.
- 위험: 번식기에 둥지 가까이 지나가는 사람에게 급강하한다. 겨울에는 드물다.
- 대응: 봄에는 둥지 밑 통로를 피해 돌아간다. 철거하면 새 횃대가 옆에 생긴다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0034"></a>
<a id="g02e02"></a>

### G02E02 · 공동 횃대 무리

- 규모·곳: 대림·가리봉 철탑, 왕십리 가선, 한강 다리 철골.
- 겨울 행동: 낮에는 흩어져 먹이터로 날고 해 질 무렵 돌아온다. 소리를 서로 배운다.
- 위험: 곡물 자루를 찢고 전선 피복을 쪼며, 횃대 밑에 배설물이 쌓인다.
- 대응: 창고 틈을 막고 횃대 밑을 치운다. 쫓으면 이웃 탑으로 옮겨 간다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0264"></a>
<a id="g02e12"></a>

### G02E12 · 이소한 어린 개체

- 규모·곳: 부모를 떠나 가까운 통학로와 지붕 위에서 새 횃대를 시험한다.
- 겨울 행동: 가장자리에서 어른의 소리를 따라 배운다.
- 위험: 경험이 없어 사람과 차량에 부딪혀 많이 죽는다.
- 대응: 길 위의 먹이를 치워 사고를 줄인다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0287"></a>
<a id="g02e13"></a>

### G02E13 · 늙은 개체

- 규모·곳: 공동 횃대의 가장자리 낮은 가지.
- 겨울 행동: 귀에 익은 소리에만 반응하고 낯선 경보에 늦는다.
- 위험: 오래 배운 소리만 따라 새 경보에 늦게 날아오른다.
- 대응: 경보를 바꿀 때는 먼저 같은 소리를 며칠 덧붙여 들려준다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0218"></a>
<a id="g02e10"></a>

### G02E10 · 대림·가리봉 철탑 군집

- 규모·곳: 영등포구 대림·가리봉과 구로의 공장 철탑. 자유도시 땅이다.
- 겨울 행동: 영등포선 지붕과 철탑에서 자고, 낮에는 공장 터와 시장 골목을 돈다.
- 위험: 절연 애자가 배설물로 덮여 합선이 나고 자유도시의 공방 전기가 끊긴다.
- 대응: 철탑 아래 배설물을 정기로 치우고 피복을 갈아 둥지 재료가 되지 않게 한다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0241"></a>
<a id="g02e11"></a>

### G02E11 · 한강 다리 횃대 군집

- 규모·곳: 한강 다리 철골. 어느 세력의 땅인지는 다리가 걸친 구에 따라 갈린다.
- 겨울 행동: 다리 위 점검구와 철골 틈에서 잔다. 아침마다 다리 위 차량이 끌고 온 먹이를 줍는다.
- 위험: 다리 위 배설물이 미끄러워 사람과 수레가 넘어진다. 급강하로 통행이 멈추기도 한다.
- 대응: 다리 점검구를 닫고 먹이 찌꺼기를 치워 횃대로 쓰기 어렵게 한다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0379"></a>

## 서식지와 겨울 대응

큰부리까마귀의 땅은 횃대가 정한다. 서쪽에서는 영등포구 대림·가리봉과 구로의 공장 철탑(자유도시 땅)이, 동쪽에서는 성동구 왕십리 가선 교차점(정부 땅)이 큰 횃대다. 한강 다리의 철골에는 다리가 닿는 구의 세력이 걸린다. 겨울이 깊어지면 서부 한강변의 곤충과 물고기 사체를 찾아 강을 따라 잠시 옮겨 간다.

자유도시는 공방과 전기를 손으로 고치는 기술자가 많아 피복을 직접 갈 수 있지만, 사람이 모자라 모든 철탑을 돌보지는 못한다. 정부는 왕십리 쪽 가선 점검에 인력을 쓰나 의사가 없어 사람이 철탑에서 떨어지면 응급 처치까지만 한다. 높은 곳에서 일하는 사람이 가장 큰 위험을 진다.

까마귀는 셔터 틈으로 들어가지 않지만, 열린 출구와 환기구 앞에 모여 음식물 집하장과 곡물 창고를 노린다. 지하 깊이 사는 사람은 셔터로 막을 수 있다. 지상에 사는 사람은 지붕과 처마 밑 곡물 자루를 직접 지켜야 한다.

좋은 대응은 먹이를 막고 횃대를 바꾸는 것이다. 곡물과 음식물을 뚜껑 있는 통에 담고, 전선 피복을 제때 갈아 둥지 재료를 없앤다. 소리는 바꾸지 않고 같은 경보를 꾸준히 쓴다. 성과는 쫓아낸 수가 아니라 합선 횟수, 곡물 손실, 통신이 끊긴 시간으로 본다.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0445"></a>

## 연결 시나리오

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0446"></a>

### G02-SC1 · 세 번의 경적

- 단계: 1
- 촉발: 겨울 아침, 왕십리 가선 교차점에서 정비 차량이 세 번 경적을 울리면 공동 횃대의 까마귀들이 같은 소리를 따라 낸다.
- 관련 세력: 정부 성동 쪽 역장, 가선 정비 일꾼, 일반 시민
- 생태 기제: 까마귀는 되풀이되는 소리를 먹이가 나오는 신호로 익혀 퍼뜨린다. 그 소리가 나면 곡물 창고 앞으로 모인다.
- 선택지: 경적 소리를 바꾼다 / 소리 대신 깃발을 쓴다 / 곡물 창고를 옮긴다
- 결과: 소리를 바꾸면 며칠 동안 오경보가 늘고 곧 새 소리를 익힌다. 깃발은 새에게 덜 퍼진다. 창고를 옮기면 일손이 든다.
- 도덕 비용: 일하는 사람의 안전과 새를 쫓는 비용 중 어느 쪽을 먼저 줄일지 정해야 한다.
- 원본 항목: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0448"></a>

### G02-SC2 · 가짜 정전 경보

- 단계: 2
- 촉발: 까마귀가 절연체를 쪼고 배설물이 쌓인 뒤, 대림 공방의 전기가 끊겨 정전 경보가 울린다.
- 관련 세력: 자유도시 대림 쪽 역장, 공방 기술자, 일반 시민
- 생태 기제: 습기와 배설물이 합선을 만들어 경보가 울리고, 까마귀는 그 소리를 먹이 신호로 배운다.
- 선택지: 절연체를 먼저 갈고 닦는다 / 경보음을 한동안 끈다 / 철탑 둘레에 접근 금지 줄을 친다
- 결과: 절연체를 갈면 전기가 돌아오지만 일손이 든다. 경보를 끄면 진짜 정전을 놓칠 수 있다. 접근 금지는 새를 못 막는다.
- 도덕 비용: 공방의 전기를 지키는 일과 위험한 철탑 작업을 맡은 사람의 안전이 부딪힌다.
- 원본 항목: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0450"></a>

### G02-SC3 · 교량 위의 새 신호

- 단계: 3
- 촉발: 한강 다리 위 점검구에 큰 횃대가 생기자 다리 위 통행이 새벽마다 끊긴다.
- 관련 세력: 다리가 닿는 구의 역장, 짐꾼과 수레꾼, 일반 시민
- 생태 기제: 다리 철골 틈이 눈과 바람을 피하는 잠자리가 되어 까마귀가 모이고, 차량이 흘린 먹이가 아침마다 새를 끈다.
- 선택지: 점검구를 닫는다 / 통행 시간을 옮긴다 / 다리 위 먹이를 치운다
- 결과: 점검구를 닫으면 횃대가 줄지만 점검 인력이 는다. 통행 시간을 옮기면 짐꾼이 추위에 오래 선다. 먹이를 치우면 새가 옆 다리로 옮겨 간다.
- 도덕 비용: 다리의 점검 노동과 새가 옮겨 가는 옆 다리의 부담을 누가 질지 정해야 한다.
- 원본 항목: G02
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, title_ko, category_id, sort, body_md, body_ko_md) VALUES ('Hostile-Group-G03', 'G03 · Feral Dog', 'G03 · 들개', 'bestiary', 3, '<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0001"></a>

[Ecology bestiary](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0003"></a>

## Feral Dogs in the Winter

Feral dogs are dogs that people once kept and lost, and their puppies. After the Great Blackout, dogs that lost their homes, dogs left behind after guarding building sites and warehouses, and dogs separated from their owners during evacuation met in the ruined streets and formed small packs. They are not mutated and do not send signals; they are ordinary dogs living while avoiding people. Memories of having been tamed remain, so they come close to people, but they fear people as much as they trust them.

A pack is a small family of two or three adults and their young. The dog that has lived longest walks ahead and starts a move first, but it has no authority to command. A family with puppies guards its own spot. In winter, with food short, several families sometimes gather loosely.

The danger comes in three forms: raiding food stores, food-waste yards, and livestock pens; hunting down people who go outside the stations at night in a group and biting them; and rabies and parasites passing to people and livestock. Rabies in particular is easy to notice too late after a bite.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0006"></a>

## Ecology

| Item | Record |
|---|---|
| Winter movement | When the cold comes, they gather where they can shelter from the wind: under elevated roads, in the car parks of surface buildings, and in closed guard passages. They move in search of food around the market alleys near Yongsan and Noryangjin Fish Market, and by day hide in drains and under elevated roads. |
| Food | They eat fish offal, food scraps, small animals, and carcasses. They depend on food people throw away, on grain, and on soup-kitchen waste. When water freezes they eat snow and gather near drains where the water does not freeze. |
| Breeding and life cycle | Females give birth in spring and autumn. In winter, with less food, puppies find it hard to survive. A lone wandering dog sometimes joins a family, and when people take some away the rest gather again at the same place. |
| Senses | Smell is sharpest. They tell human scent, disinfectant, and thawing meat apart. By hearing they recognize cart sounds and engines idling, and they remember when the feeding cart comes. The jingle of collars and tags against each other is a marker that tells individuals apart. |
| Pack behavior | The dog that has lived longest decides a move first, but some dogs do not follow. A family with puppies guards its own. When a few bark, nearby dogs rush in and in an instant look like a large pack, but they soon scatter by family. |
| Disease and danger | They carry rabies, canine distemper, and parasites. Rabies appears weeks after a bite, so it is noticed late, and once symptoms start it is hard to save the person. On the Government side with no doctors, the White Cross gives only first aid for bites. At night, when several dogs corner one person, the injuries are serious. |
| Response principles | Seal the food firmly, stay away from families with puppies, and do not go out alone at night. If a person is bitten, wash the wound at once and arrange rabies vaccination. If all the dogs are taken, the rest go deeper in search of food. Results are judged not by how many were caught but by the number of people bitten and the number of store intrusions. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0008"></a>

## Packs and Individuals

Below are the feral dogs that come down close to people in winter, set out as packs and individuals.

| Entry | Type | Size | Winter risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| [G03E01 · Family pack](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e01) | Pack | Two or three adults and their puppies | Store raids and night attacks |
| [G03E02 · Stray dog](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e02) | Individual | One adult dog | Solo attacks |
| [G03E07 · Mother with puppies](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e07) | Family | One mother and three to six puppies | Defensive attacks to protect puppies |
| [G03E13 · Old dog](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e13) | Individual | Over ten years old | Starving approaches |
| [G03E12 · Dog with a stuck collar](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e12) | Individual | One adult dog | Collar entanglement |
| [G03E16 · Noryangjin Fish Market population](/wiki/Hostile-Group-G03/#g03e16) | Regional population | Several dozen animals | Occupation of unloading yards |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0011"></a>
<a id="g03e01"></a>

### G03E01 · Family pack

- Size and place: Around the market alleys at Yongsan and the unloading yards toward Noryangjin, and under elevated roads.
- Winter behavior: The dog that has lived longest goes first and the puppies wait in a safe spot. At night they circle the feeding grounds.
- Danger: They raid grain stores and collection yards. They drive down and bite people who come out alone at night.
- Response: Seal the food and do not walk alone at night. Do not approach the puppies.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0034"></a>
<a id="g03e02"></a>

### G03E02 · Stray dog

- Size and place: It wanders along roadside drains and under elevated roads.
- Winter behavior: A dog that people lost goes alone, still wearing a collar ring. It looks for old feeding places by smell.
- Danger: When hungry it will rush a person carrying a bag of food.
- Response: Do not hold a food bag in front of you or run; back away slowly.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0149"></a>
<a id="g03e07"></a>

### G03E07 · Mother with puppies

- Size and place: Dry corners in the car parks of surface buildings and under elevated roads.
- Winter behavior: She hides the puppies and fetches food. When it is cold she moves them.
- Danger: A person who comes near the puppies is attacked fiercely.
- Response: Go well around the puppies’ place. Do not touch the puppies.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0337"></a>
<a id="g03e13"></a>

### G03E13 · Old dog

- Size and place: It lives at the edge of the pack.
- Winter behavior: Its teeth are worn and it cannot eat hard food, so it clings to soup kitchens near people.
- Danger: It follows people but may be sick.
- Response: Do not feed it. If illness is suspected, do not approach and report it.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0304"></a>
<a id="g03e12"></a>

### G03E12 · Dog with a stuck collar

- Size and place: It moves about with its collar ring caught on wire.
- Winter behavior: It goes about wearing a rusted collar and tag. The caught collar slows it down.
- Danger: An injured dog is quick to bite.
- Response: Do not try to free the collar alone. If it must be freed, do it with two people.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0438"></a>
<a id="g03e16"></a>

### G03E16 · Noryangjin Fish Market population

- Size and place: Around Noryangjin Fish Market and its unloading yards. This is Free City land in Dongjak-gu.
- Winter behavior: They gather in the early dawn when the market opens and scavenge fish offal and ice scraps.
- Danger: They surround unloading workers and rummage through their loads.
- Response: Split the unloading hours and keep fish offal in lidded containers.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0471"></a>

## Habitat and Winter Response

A feral dog’s territory is set by food and shelter from the wind. In the north it is the market alleys of Yongsan and closed guard passages; in the south, Noryangjin Fish Market and its unloading yards and the undersides of the elevated roads around them. Yongsan to the north is Government land, and Noryangjin is Free City land in Dongjak-gu. In other parts of the Free City south of the Han River they circle workshop back alleys and the surroundings of depots, and in Arc land in Gangnam they look for rubbish heaps around station exits.

Dogs get in through shutter gaps, vents, and under bars. The deep underground levels can be sealed with shutters, but people who live on the ground floor and above must rely on a single closed door, and workers who go out at night carry the greatest risk. In the Government the White Cross gives only first aid to bitten people, while in the Free Cities there are doctors who can give vaccination and wound treatment.

Good response means sealing the food and separating the times and routes where people meet dogs. Close food-waste yards and grain stores, and fix the times that unloading and feeding carts run, and you will know when dogs gather. Rounding them up pushes the remaining dogs deeper in, so it is a last resort. When a person is bitten, wash the wound at once and move them to a place that can vaccinate.

Results are not measured by the number caught. Look at how many people were bitten, how many times stores were entered, and how long unloading was blocked. If intrusions fall but dog traces vanish with them, that may mean not stability but deepening hunger.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0538"></a>

## Linked scenarios

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0539"></a>

### G03-SC1 · The Rusted Name Tag

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: On a snowy morning in the Yongsan market alleys, a dog wearing a rusted collar tag snatches a food sack and bolts.
- Actors: Government stationmaster in Yongsan, White Cross, ordinary citizens
- Ecological mechanism: A dog that people once kept remembers the place and time of the soup kitchen and returns to the same spot.
- Choices: Hang food sacks high / Fix one gathering place for the dogs / Read the tag to find the owner
- Outcomes: Hanging sacks moves the dogs to other alleys. Gathering them in one place fixes where people get hurt. Reading the tag takes time but the dog may be released.
- Moral cost: Safety of people and keeping the dogs alive collide over which to settle first.
- Source dossier: G03

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0541"></a>

### G03-SC2 · The Band That Remembers the Feeding Cart

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: At the unloading yards of Noryangjin Fish Market, a pack of feral dogs gathers at the hour the feeding cart arrives each time, delaying unloading.
- Actors: Free City stationmaster in Noryangjin, unloading workers, fish-market traders
- Ecological mechanism: Dogs remember the cart’s engine sound and the smell of thawing together with the hour, and when that hour comes the pack gathers.
- Choices: Change the cart’s hour / Keep offal in lidded containers / Move the unloading place
- Outcomes: Changing the hour leaves the dogs wandering for a few days. Using containers reduces food and the dogs go elsewhere. Moving the place costs money.
- Moral cost: Decide who bears the safety of the unloading workers, the burden of market labor, and the risk spreading to the next alley.
- Source dossier: G03

<a id="Hostile-Group-G03-heading-0545"></a>

### G03-SC3 · Rebinding on the Transfer Stairs

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: On a snowy night, dogs that had scattered gather again under the transfer stairs at Yongsan Station and block the stairs.
- Actors: Government stationmaster of Yongsan Station, White Cross, ordinary citizens transferring
- Ecological mechanism: In the cold several families gather under the stairs, where there is shelter from the wind, and their barking calls still more dogs.
- Choices: Block under the stairs with a temporary partition / Leave blankets and food there to lead them to one place / Cut off night passage
- Outcomes: A partition drives the dogs elsewhere. Gathering them in one place fixes where people get bitten. Cutting off passage slows transfers.
- Moral cost: The safety of people transferring and a living place for dogs shivering in the cold cannot both be provided.
- Source dossier: G03
