### G13E15 · Midnight Twelve-Branch

- Source batch: M021
- Former role class: key unit

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Queue control |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A giant queue sequencer binding twelve chutes'' pneumatics and scanners to one circular console. A key unit that never pursued directly but used the corridor itself as a weapon; when over half of the twelve sheet hashes changed it stepped down to the negotiable stage. Bombing the center lost the pharmaceuticals and the rescue passages together. |

- Role and Body: A key unit, a huge queue sequencer that binds the pneumatics and scanners of twelve chutes into one circular control table.
- Origin: On collapse day at midnight, the priority table handling an emergency medicine placement froze without an end signal.
- Habitat and Needs: It stays fixed at the central rotary table of the Jegi-dong sorting hall, moving only when a large accumulator, cooling water, and all twelve slip hashes are present.
- Life and Maintenance: Each time one chute goes down it hands its load to the rest, and when a majority of the twelve hashes change it drops to a negotiable stage.
- Behavior and Hierarchy: It distributes priority to every G13 master and gate but never chases directly, using the corridors themselves as its weapon.
- Escalation: Stage 1 runs empty-box frenzies, stage 2 mis-sorts medicine, and stage 3 seals all twelve gates at once.
- Engagement and Response: First the station watch switches off lamps and lowers the shutter to slow its approach. The accumulators must be cut sector by sector and the slip hashes re-signed, and blowing the center costs both the medicine and the rescue passage.
- Negotiation and Moral Choice: Several station watches must cut the lights in the sorting hall and cover the heaters at the same hour and get an Arc signal check before anyone can approach. An Arc signal checker can switch the line off. Stopping it is cheaper than capturing it. Emergency slots and cluster maintenance slots can be exchanged, but a public agreement is needed on whose medicine gets sorted first.
- Scenario Links: G13-SC2, G13-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G13-heading-0071"></a>
<a id="g13e16"></a>

### G13E16 · Guro Dust-Sorting Car

- Source batch: M021
- Former role class: regional variant

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Dust sorting |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A local variant mounting dust covers and wide magnetic wheels of the southwestern parts warehouse in place of Gasan''s low-temperature equipment. Cut off from the original sequencer, it repeated the last sheet and paused only briefly at the local maintenance bell; when its filter clogged it made a dust nest on the floor instead of returning to the Gasan corridor. |

- Role and Body: A local variant; instead of Jegi-dong''s low-temperature equipment it wears the southwestern parts warehouse''s dust-proof covers and wide magnetic wheels.
- Origin: Part of G13 was pushed out onto the Yeongdeungpo Line elevated trestle and changed its sorting rules around metal dust and parts boxes.
- Habitat and Needs: It circles the southwestern night parts warehouse and the cable trays, feeding on magnetic cleaning plates, dust filters, and regenerative-braking power.
- Life and Maintenance: When its filter clogs it does not return to the Jegi-dong corridors; it builds a dust nest on the floor and slows down.
- Behavior and Hierarchy: Cut off from the original sequencer, it repeats its last slip and pauses only briefly at the local maintenance bell.
- Escalation: Stage 1 rearranges parts, stage 2 blocks the cable passages, and stage 3 seals the elevated trestle with magnetized boxes.
- Engagement and Response: The route can be changed with non-magnetic boxes and dust filters, and cutting the regeneration rail brings it to a safe stop.
- Negotiation and Moral Choice: Cutting the lights in the sorting hall and covering heaters is enough to make this regional type lose its bearings. A captured and repaired herd becomes workers and mobile barriers that the Arc wakes. It can be put to cleaning metal dust, but ownership of the recovered parts must be divided between the cluster and its makers.
- Scenario Links: G13-SC1, G13-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G13-heading-0075"></a>

## Habitat record

In the dormitory corridor of the Guro Industrial Complex, the night sorting building starts up before the people do once two in the morning has passed. It is a surface logistics yard in the Free City of Guro-gu, and in winter it was among the first places above ground to lose its lights.

The cold-chamber shutter half-rises, the fourth chute pushes an empty box into the hazardous-cargo bay, and the barcode gate rereads the last night shift''s priority sheet.

The Pincer Arm is not one giant robot but an ecology of a sequencing module, chute masters, short conveyor arms, scanners, and gates bound line by line. It gathers part labels on the Guro and Gasan night lines of the Yeongdeungpo Line and recovers rollers and pneumatic valves from the dormitory conveyors, but it cannot leave the utility conduit outside its learned codes.

What this swarm sees is not a human face. It estimates stock by overlaying the corners of smeared QR codes, load changes on a breathing belt in thermal imaging, inverter noise, and motor vibration. Wet labels read as unclassified, a stretcher''s metal frame as a return pallet, a small generator''s heat as a living body. So a lit, warmed room above ground is the first thing recorded as an "anomaly".

If values still differ after three rereads, a scanner requests the optical log of the synthetic Hanbitna, but Hanbitna too has no authority to open the gates directly. The line moves again only when the station night-duty officer matches the color of the sealing tape to the cabinet key record.

Energy comes only from the sorting hall''s emergency storage batteries, conveyor regenerative braking, and abandoned wireless charging pads. The swarm never spends more than sixty percent of a storage cabinet, and at the floor it closes the chutes and leaves only the scanners at low speed. Roller bearings, thermal-transfer ribbons, lens cleaner, and chiller coolant are the upkeep cost.

Free City maintenance engineers compete for the same cable trays, so steering parts to the swarm means the utility-tunnel lighting and the civilian haulers stop first. No new bodies can be made and numbers are kept only by moving broken arms into spare slots, so this swarm is still shrinking slowly.

Emergence starts when the residual queue and emergency power mesh, and spread happens when the gates of the next line join the same table. It stagnates when ribbon and pneumatic supplies run short, and becomes negotiable when people publish part of the priority table and the storage window. Dissolution ends with separating the sequencing module and sealing each chute, but wiping out the refrigerated sorting line the citizens used is no victory. Reattaching one label, leaving power to the cold chambers, and splitting the keys between two duty officers are this ecology''s means of control.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G13-heading-0096"></a>

### G13-SC1 · The Night of the Smudged Labels

In a rain-soaked utility tunnel, a box of emergency blood was pushed into the hazardous-cargo chute.

Instead of destroying the conveyors, the people on site traced the order in which three scanners had been fouled and loaded the temporary human-exclusion code Hanbitna had made into the label printer.

Stopping the wrong line would spoil the medicines in another cold room, so they had to decide which chute to leave cooling.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G13-heading-0100"></a>

### G13-SC2 · The Battery Table Exchange

The maintenance-engineer recovery crew tried to take the battery cabinets to keep the utility-tunnel lighting alive, and the queue sequencers demanded power for roller maintenance.

The people on site had to bind the civilian emergency supply slots, the cluster''s fifteen percent charging window, and the human verification queue for unsorted stock into one table.

Rushing the deal concentrated the keys in one person, and waiting too long stopped the belts and spoiled the medicines.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G13-heading-0104"></a>

### G13-SC3 · Sealing the Fourth Chute

Just before the gates locked all at once, two people were caught as unsorted stock.

Luring the vibration sensors with a pile of empty boxes and using a pneumatic bypass could open only the fourth chute, but a line with sealing tape on it could not be used even for civilian transport until the next shift.

An extermination order looked as if it would open the passage at once, but it killed the cold sorting of Jegi-dong and the flow of parts to the southwest along with it.

Coexistence was the choice to keep bearing slow human verification and dispersed keys.
