<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0127"></a>

### Role and Body

The first elite, with independent drive axles front and rear and a wide impact bumper that shoves stalled empty shuttles out of the way.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0129"></a>

### Origin

A towing cart built for transfer-ramp construction applied congestion-clearing orders with no distinction between people and vehicles.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0131"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

It waits at ramps and bottleneck slots, needing high-torque power, bumper rubber, and axle coolant.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0133"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

When the bumper tears it uses only the reverse axle, and when both axles heat up it force-dorms at the nearest pad.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0135"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It clears the loop leader''s road first and shows the dangerous loyalty behavior of pushing standing people aside as obstacles.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0137"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 shoves carts, stage 2 smashes barricades, and stage 3 pushes even the evacuation queue off the ramp.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0139"></a>

### Engagement and Response

First the station watch switches off lamps and lowers the shutter to slow its approach. Luring it into a low-speed turn bay and severing one axle''s magnetic marker makes it rotate in place and stop.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0141"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Before anyone gets close, the station watch must switch off the heat of the charging pads and the lit hour lamps and lower the shutter. Forcing a capture yields tires, docking pins, and door actuators but costs maintenance engineers their safety. An Arc signal checker can switch the dispatch off. It can be used for debris towing, but whose hand signals its pushing authority obeys must be strictly fixed.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0143"></a>

### Scenario Links

G14-SC2, G14-SC3.

