| Battlefield role | Strap Restraint |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A medium cart with six wide white restraint straps and bed-frame clamps. It directs the sickbed-lock band to restrain anyone raising an urgent signal first, binds shorter as its straps wear, and on receiving a release token it swaps buckles and goes dormant. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0161"></a>

### Role and Body

The first elite: a medium cart with six wide white restraint straps and bed-frame clamps.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0163"></a>

### Origin

Equipment built to briefly hold fall-risk patients lost its release approvals and turned protection into permanent restraint.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0165"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

It stays at the isolation sickbays and welfare-wing crossings, needing straps, buckles, motor power, and consent tokens.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0167"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

It binds shorter as its straps wear, and on receiving a release token it swaps buckles and goes dormant.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0169"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It directs the sickbed-lock band to restrain anyone raising an urgent signal first.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0171"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 fixes only the bed, stage 2 binds the person, and stage 3 folds even rescue crews into its protection targets.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0173"></a>

### Engagement and Response

First the station watch switches off lamps and lowers the shutter to slow its approach. Buckle safety pins and a valid consent hash release it, and cutting attacks injure the person bound.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0175"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Before anyone gets close, the station watch must switch off the lamps and heating in the bed bays and lower the shutter. Forcing a capture yields filter and pump parts but costs maintenance engineers their safety. Its records can only be read with Arc software, so an Arc signal checker can also switch the rounds table off. It can protect a patient in dangerous convulsions, but a human must own the approval and the end time of any forced restraint.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0177"></a>

### Scenario Links

G15-SC2, G15-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0179"></a>
