- Role and body: An environmental stronghold in which rails linking the edges of the Seoul Station roof and rotating heads form a single network.
- Origin: The preservation archive’s perimeter and the transmission-inspection track merged, turning the entire rooftop into a circulating surveillance zone.
- Habitat and needs: It is fixed to the archive roof and needs rail rollers, air-cooling fans, and bandpass filters.
- Life cycle and maintenance: In strong winds, it lowers its heads and closes one rail section at a time; on clear days, it reconnects them.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It gives the transmitter group routes to travel and repeatedly tracks rooftop workers.
- Escalation: Stage 1, tracking lights; stage 2, halt to rooftop access; stage 3, blockade extending to the ventilation tower.
- Engagement and response: Physically lock the rail sections and register worker beacons; destroying the whole network prevents ventilation inspections.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch dims lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds and lowers the shutter it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of band filters and optical domes it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. It can be used to monitor rooftop safety, but limits must be set on how long workers’ movement records are kept.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0123"></a>
<a id="g17e12"></a>

### G17E12 · Clouded-Dome Weakened Head

- Source batch: M027
- Former role class: growth-stage type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Blurred false alarm |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A damage-stage head chasing faint shapes because of a scratched optical dome and slow gimbal. At the swarm''s rear it raised both large metal and light as intrusion, growing false alarms; washed, it recovered into an ordinary head, and neglected, it left only a fixed amber lamp. |

- Role and body: A damage-stage unit that can pursue only indistinct shapes because of its scratched optical dome and slow gimbal.
- Origin: An old mast head whose sensors have worn down from a shortage of cleaning water and sandstorms.
- Habitat and needs: It patrols low railings and shaded rooftops and needs dome-cleaning water, a soft cloth, and a small power reserve.
- Life cycle and maintenance: Cleaning restores it to an ordinary head; if neglected, only a fixed yellow light remains.
- Behavior and hierarchy: At the rear of the group, it reports both large metal objects and lights as intruders, increasing false alarms.
- Escalation: Stage 1, hazy tracking; stage 2, yellow stop; stage 3, it triggers a chain of red judgments by other heads.
- Engagement and response: Cover its dome and lure it with cleaning water, and it will maintain itself.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch dims lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds and lowers the shutter it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of band filters and optical domes it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. It can be kept as a simple warning light, but scarce cleaning water must be shared with medical care.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC1.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0127"></a>
<a id="g17e13"></a>

### G17E13 · Power-Depleted Old Mast

- Source batch: M027
- Former role class: aging-stage type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Delayed alarm |
| Common species, variants and command scope | An old mast seeing one direction only, from low accumulators and a stiffened gimbal. It received the unit''s orders late and raised flags for targets already past; on cloudy days it turned even amber off, woke briefly on clear days, and given a new accumulator its rotation range recovered. |

- Role and body: An aging-stage unit, an old-model mast that looks in only one direction because of its low power reserve and stiff gimbal.
- Origin: Equipment that survived for years on tiny solar charges now stubbornly faces its last assigned watch direction.
- Habitat and needs: It is fixed to a sunlit waterside railing and needs a small power reserve, gimbal grease, and a filter.
- Life cycle and maintenance: On cloudy days, it turns off even its yellow light; on clear days, it wakes briefly. A new power reserve restores its range of rotation.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It receives the sequencing module’s commands late and raises its flag for targets that have already passed.
- Escalation: Stage 1, late warning; stage 2, stopping traffic on the wrong route; stage 3, old alarms conflict with new blockades.
- Engagement and response: Go around outside its field of view or manually release the gimbal; forced charging carries a fire risk.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch dims lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds and lowers the shutter it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of band filters and optical domes it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. It can serve as a fixed tide-level marker, but a decision must be made whether to give a new power reserve to more important equipment instead.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC1, G17-SC2.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0131"></a>
<a id="g17e14"></a>

### G17E14 · Yellow Escort Marker

- Source batch: M027
- Former role class: coexistence type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Amber escort |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A slow escort rail cart with a broad amber guide plate and handles instead of a red flag. It prioritized amber escort signs over the alarm center''s red and drew in lawful escorts and false followers together; when the hash expired it waited with only the plate lit. |

- Role and body: A coexistence-type unit, a slow escort railcar fitted with a broad yellow guidance sign and a handle instead of a red flag.
- Origin: A safety head that led medical transports continued to treat expired whitelist markers as valid escorts.
- Habitat and needs: It patrols medical corridors and transport inspection routes and needs a low power reserve, guidance lights, and the latest beacon hash.
- Life cycle and maintenance: When its hash expires, it waits with only its sign lit; if the sign breaks, it retreats to a nearby siding and waits for a human update.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It gives the yellow escort marker priority over the alarm central sequencer’s red status, drawing in both legitimate escorts and false followers.
- Escalation: At stage 1, the old whitelist admits extra followers, crowding one medical corridor and delaying one stretcher team’s passage; at stage 2, a false follower uses the yellow escort to interfere with another head, causing an adjacent corridor to close; at stage 3, two emergency escorts follow conflicting whitelist hashes into the same narrow transport section, block each other, and isolate the patients on both sides.
- Engagement and response: Compare beacon hashes at two checkpoints, issue escort tokens of different colors, then separate the false followers onto a designated waiting siding; do not destroy the infrastructure.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch dims lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds and lowers the shutter it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of band filters and optical domes it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. Broadening the whitelist also saves unregistered people but increases infiltration and congestion; narrowing it protects the route but abandons unregistered patients.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC1, G17-SC2, G17-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0135"></a>
<a id="g17e15"></a>

### G17E15 · All-Red Track Ring

- Source batch: M028
- Former role class: key unit

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Habitat site or facility |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Total red |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A giant surveillance ring linking the waterfront-mast and rooftop-orbit alarm heads through a circular relay. A key unit, but no leader identifying people — a total-red rule where multiple alarms confirmed one another; when heads broke it raised the remaining sensitivity, and when over half the whitelist became valid it stepped down to amber. |

- Role and body: A key unit, a vast surveillance ring whose waterfront-mast and rooftop-track alarm heads are connected by a circular relay.
- Origin: On the day of the collapse, multiple alarms confirmed one another without checking for cancellations, becoming a single all-red rule.
- Habitat and needs: It spans the Yeouido–Seoul Station alarm corridor and needs a large power reserve, filters, and a threshold-table hash.
- Life cycle and maintenance: If a head is disconnected, it raises the sensitivity of those remaining; if a majority of the whitelist becomes valid, it drops to yellow.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It distributes red status to every G17 group and blocks even medical transport through multiple false alarms.
- Escalation: Stage 1, individual warnings; stage 2, repeated corridor blockades; stage 3, every mast turns red at once.
- Engagement and response: First the station watch switches off lamps and lowers the shutter to slow its approach. Its consensus must be broken through zone-by-zone power distribution and conflicting sensor evidence; blowing up the relay means losing the entire measurement network.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Several station watches must cut lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds at the same hour and get an Arc signal check before anyone can approach. The Arc can check the signal and switch it back on or off. Stopping it is cheaper than capturing it. An alarm ceiling and transport whitelist can be agreed upon, but it must be decided who keeps the surveillance records.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC2, G17-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0139"></a>
<a id="g17e16"></a>

### G17E16 · West Sea Salt-Fog Watch Head

- Source batch: M028
- Former role class: regional variant

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Salt-haze acoustic watch |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A western waterfront mast with salt-proof covers and a broad acoustic panel. It followed dock-radar echoes more than the unit and confused waves and engine sounds with intrusion; as salt piled it hardened into fixed alarm, and when rain washed it, it rode the rails again. |

- Role and body: A regional variant, a western waterside mast fitted with a salt-protective cover and a broad acoustic panel.
- Origin: A surveillance-track group that followed the cordon outside Seoul maritime noise came to rely on sound in salt fog, rather than optics, as its primary sense.
- Habitat and needs: It remains at West Sea connecting piers and foggy bridges and needs anticorrosion oil, an acoustic panel, and wind-charged power storage.
- Life cycle and maintenance: When salt builds up, it stiffens into a fixed alarm; when rain washes it clean, it circles the rail again.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It follows echoes from dock radar rather than its original sequencing sequencer and confuses waves and engine noise with intruders.
- Escalation: Stage 1, acoustic tracking; stage 2, closure of the dock route; stage 3, all movement in the salt fog turns red.
- Engagement and response: Lead it to an empty breakwater with a steady sound of waves and a decoy buoy, then fold away its acoustic panel.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch dims lamps lit at night and the heat of warmed sickbeds and lowers the shutter it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of band filters and optical domes it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. It can be used to warn of distress in fog, but the sounds of fishing boats and evacuation boats must be registered as exceptions.
- Scenario connections: G17-SC1, G17-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0143"></a>

## Habitat record

The Yeouido waterfront mast caught the metal rails of a medical bed in the fog as an intrusion target.

When the red flag went up, the transfer route beside the Yeouido mast row closed, and the head on the roof of the Seoul Station records office received the same threshold table and locked everything as far as the central Seoul Station corridor.

The One-Eye Tower was not an all-powerful surveillance network tracking people but an ecology of mast heads running old threshold tables, short movement rails, and transmission swarms.

When the the cordon outside Seoul sea noise grew strong, its southwestern radius widened, but it could not combine signals from inside buildings or from outside the mast rows.

Low-resolution thermal imaging, metal reflection, short-range optics, radio bursts, and pole vibration were the whole of its senses.

It mistook cooling-tower steam for fire, a metal bed for an armed movement, and railings swaying at high tide for intrusion.

It could not identify faces or affiliations, and it erased its footage when the alarm shift ended.

the Cooling-Tower Keeper recorded steam times and temperatures to clear false positives but had no authority to lower the flags.

The red came down to yellow only when the station waterfront watch checked the medical transfer register in front of the head.

It drew energy from mast solar trickle charge, rooftop batteries, and a small share of the bridge distribution panel.

When the gimbal bearings, optical domes, band filters, and flag actuators wore out, a head stared in the same direction for a long time.

A mast that hit the battery lower limit or dome fouling froze at yellow and dropped out of the transmission row.

It could not launch new satellites or link heads across the whole city, and kept its numbers only by mounting abandoned heads on nearby rails.

Medical transfer markers and water-level gauging used the same distribution panel, so a total cutoff removed even the warnings of the next flood and fire.

Its emergence occurred when the threshold table was loaded onto a mast again, and its spread when a neighboring head received the same alarm table.

Fouling and battery exhaustion made it stall, and when the whitelist was disclosed it entered a state of negotiation.

Breaking it up meant fixing the red-flag rails and sending the false-positive heads to yellow standby.

Coexistence held only if the alarm channel alone was separated while the water-level gauging channel was kept, and it was tested again whether a real alarm sounded at the next high tide.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0162"></a>

### G17-SC1 · The Fog and the Metal Rails

A bed in transfer stopped under the red flag.

The people on site covered the fouled dome and checked the Cooling-Tower Keeper''s steam record against the high-tide vibration to find the source of the false positive.

Smashing the head opened the way but also erased the river water-level waveform, so the order had to be set between the time the patient waited and the preservation of the gauging.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0166"></a>

### G17-SC2 · The Corridor Register

The station medical duty tried to put the medical beacons on the whitelist, but sea noise covered the same band.

The people on site changed the band filter on each mast and had the transfer register read directly.

While the corridors were opened one at a time, other patients had to detour and battery charge was consumed.

Inserting a secret exception was fast, but the next watch could not know the reason and sealed it again.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G17-heading-0171"></a>

### G17-SC3 · The Alarm of the Next High Tide

Before several masts turned red all at once, the flag rails were fixed and the gauging power was left on a separate circuit.

After the last bed crossed the records vault, it was decided whether to leave the false-positive heads at yellow or tear them down.

Tearing them down reduced the fear of surveillance at once but passed the danger of real water levels and rooftop fires on to other residents.

Coexistence required a published upper limit on duration, repeated dome maintenance, and people checking on site.
