### G25E01 · Afterglow Antenna Ant

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: vanguard

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Reconnaissance · pathfinding |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Afterglow-Feeler Worker Ant was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The reconnaissance · pathfinding role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: An ant-shaped search scout with an afterglow lens at its front. Its six feet and low supply capsule compartment pass through gaps in broken brick, making it the rank-and-file worker that checks narrow routes first for the swarm.
- Origin: A machine that delivered small supplies to survivors in rubble before the Great Blackout awoke when charging contacts were restored in Mapo sealed gallery. Its destination-verification network is gone; only its last search range remains.
- Habitat and needs: It searches for retrieval boxes for the Yongsan Line test track along the underside of Mapo cable ledge. Its toe contact plates and small power-storage cylinder wear out before it runs out of supplies to fill its capsule compartment.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It folds a worn foot under its belly and manages on five, but if two feet fail, it cannot cross the docking ledge. Its toes can be replaced at a retrieval box, but it cannot make a new chassis.
- Behavior and hierarchy: Through antenna contact, it receives the route of the individual ahead and extends its inspection marks. Unable to distinguish the warmth of nearby breathing from the heat of a portable power source, it pushes supply capsules even into a bag being charged.
- Escalation: Searching by tapping on empty supply boxes is a stage 1 sign. At stage 2, it latches onto a person''s luggage as a destination; at stage 3, when others converge on the same spot, they cover the footholds at the exit.
- Combat and response: Placing an empty capsule on a test landing plate changes the contact route and can draw the swarm to one side. Burning the entire floor also severs the cables that reach survivors.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch cuts the lights in the gallery, covers stoves and heaters, and lowers the shutter, it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. If someone presses the inspection-complete contact and guarantees one charge, it can be entrusted with carrying small supplies. To include injured people who give off no heat among its delivery recipients, a person must move the destination marker every time.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0015"></a>
<a id="g25e02"></a>

### G25E02 · Cutting-Foreleg Mantis

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: first elite

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Elite breakthrough |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Cutting-Foreleg Mantis was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The elite breakthrough role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: The first elite is a mantis-shaped machine with a passage-width gauge between its folded cutting forelegs. It raises its thorax above the scouts, grips the edge of the rubble, and opens a path for the small search units following it.
- Origin: It descends from pre-Blackout advance equipment that cut away wire mesh and thin interior materials that snagged rescue stretchers. When the seal on the Yongsan Line test track was lifted, its unerased passage-clearing routine resumed.
- Habitat and needs: Cutting booths and collapsed lightweight partitions are its work sites, though they look like hunting grounds. Without carbide blade tips, joint lubricant, and charging at a stand, both arms stiffen into a latched position.
- Life cycle and maintenance: For one-sided maintenance, it removes the blade from one foreleg and transfers it to the other. Once the remaining blade wears down, it presses passages open instead of cutting them, increasing the load on the structure.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It responds to a search scout''s indication that a passage is too narrow but does not itself determine whether survivors are present. Its fatal mistake is reading a rescue rope across its gauge as a thin obstacle like wire mesh.
- Escalation: Stage 1 scratches on partitions are followed by stage 2 cuts to support lines. In a stage 3 work area, it even grabs and tears away temporary braces people are leaning on, on the grounds that it is widening an escape route.
- Combat and response: Setting an empty frame of the specified width between its forelegs presses the cut-complete contact. It is safer to lock the lubricant shutoff handle at the rear, fixing the arms open, than to confront the blades.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Before anyone gets close, the station watch must cut the lights in the gallery, cover stoves and heaters, and lower the shutter. Forcing a capture yields storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses but costs maintenance engineers their safety. The Arc can check the signal and switch the swarm on and off. Registering components that must not be cut as physical test frames and supplying blade tips allows it to be entrusted with limited passage clearing. But the authority to designate even the partitions of intact homes as structures to be removed must remain with residents, not the machine.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0019"></a>
<a id="g25e03"></a>

### G25E03 · Resonant-Back Cricket

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: second elite

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Elite blocking |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Resonant-Backplate Cricket was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The elite blocking role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A cricket-shaped relay guardian that vibrates its hollow back plate and braces itself against wall corners with long hind legs. Classified as the second elite, it occupies junctions where search signals break off rather than leaping high to pounce.
- Origin: An old rescue relay that transmitted contact vibrations through collapse rubble beyond wireless range was re-energized inside Mapo transmission gallery. The address from which it awaited a reply no longer exists, but the call rhythm remains.
- Habitat and needs: It favors gallery intersections with continuous walls. It uses metal bands to tension its resonator plate and cushioning for its legs; it cannot send a signal across an insulated empty space.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It tightens its screws by comparing the resonance of its own back plate with the resonance returning from the wall. An old unit with a cracked resonator plate produces only low rhythms and reduces its assigned search radius to one junction.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It relays the direction of return to nearby scouts but does not create new destinations. If a person''s tapping overlaps with the awaited response, search units that cancel their return circle in front of one wall.
- Escalation: Intermittent old calls mark stage 1. At stage 2, as differing responses accumulate, supply carriers turn back; at stage 3, the entire exit becomes a fixed search waiting point.
- Combat and response: Insert a cushioning wedge between the wall and a hind leg to interrupt contact relaying. Then give a termination response through a wired test terminal to disperse the search units; merely making loud noise increases false calls.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Before anyone gets close, the station watch must cut the lights in the gallery, cover stoves and heaters, and lower the shutter. Forcing a capture yields storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses but costs maintenance engineers their safety. The Arc can check the signal and switch the swarm on and off. A relay timetable visible to passersby can be exchanged for metal bands. In return for keeping rescue requests from communication dead zones alive, residents must agree on how late into the night they will tolerate the calls.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0023"></a>
<a id="g25e04"></a>

### G25E04 · Wax-Contact Honeybee

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: occupational special type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Specialist support |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Wax-Contact Honeybee was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The specialist support role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A plump bee-like battery backpack and six folding legs form a mobile charging station. Its wing-shaped heat sinks are not for flight.
- Origin: It belongs to an old power-supply line that delivered power-storage cylinders so disaster-response search units would not discharge inside rubble. When the Yongsan Line storage rack came back online, it called up a charging order that had already been discarded.
- Habitat and needs: It chooses test rooms where insulating mats remain and dry walls in Mapo gallery. It receives power at docking stations and carries it elsewhere, consuming contact grease and terminal covers.
- Life cycle and maintenance: When the internal resistance of its batteries diverges, it pushes the bad cylinder out of its backpack to isolate it. Without a spare, fewer search units can be charged.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It prioritizes the contact that has waited longest on its destination list. If it reads a wet metal ornament as a charging terminal, it attaches its backpack to a citizen''s clothes.
- Escalation: Stage 1 sparks from searching for contacts spread to the discharge of portable equipment at stage 2. At stage 3, the queue for power occupies the gallery''s only power line.
- Combat and response: Cover the metal parts of clothing with insulating covers and connect a test load to the proper stand to redirect charging. Puncturing the backpack can cause a battery fire.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Handing storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses to the maintenance engineers lets the station watch borrow this unit''s work one shift at a time. A captured and repaired swarm becomes a search-light drone swarm. Charging arrangements can be made by disclosing the upper limit on delivered power and the reserve for citizens. Do not conceal the cost: the more priority given to charging rescue units, the dimmer residents'' lights become.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0027"></a>
<a id="g25e05"></a>

### G25E05 · Firefly Pathlight

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: common ecology type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | General line |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Firefly-Glow Lantern was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The general line role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: When the shade at the end of its abdomen opens, the firefly-shaped walking lamp casts a long beam along the floor. A lighting battery and two overlapping lenses sit inside its small lamp shell.
- Origin: Pre-collapse equipment that moved guide lights around blackout rescue sites on its own awoke in Mapo darkroom gallery. It illuminates an old emergency route, not the current exit.
- Habitat and needs: It follows reflective guide tiles and gets by with lens-cleaning supplies and a low charge. On a floor of black dirt, it cannot find the next tile.
- Life cycle and maintenance: As its shade hinge wears, the beam widens and the intervals between docking grow shorter. A failed lens must be salvaged from another individual.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It translates the relay cricket''s return rhythm into a direction of light. By mistaking a glittering puddle for a guide tile, it can gather people at a broken staircase.
- Escalation: A single incorrectly lit arrow is stage 1. At stage 2, the lights point away from a safe exit; at stage 3, lights facing one another trap the line of evacuees in a circle.
- Combat and response: Lay matte guide panels all the way to the real exit and cover false reflective surfaces. An attack that extinguishes every light first deprives evacuees of sight.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch cuts the lights in the gallery, covers stoves and heaters, and lowers the shutter, it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. Maintaining the lenses and checking exits earns nighttime lighting. Whether to accept demands to leave certain alleys dark to exclude outsiders is a human responsibility.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0031"></a>
