### G25E09 · Stretcher-Carrying Mantis

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: occupational special type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Specialist support |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Satchel-Holding Mantis was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The specialist support role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: Capture forelegs fitted with soft straps extend below its mantis-shaped thorax. With stretcher clasps instead of blades, it holds moving loads, unlike the cutting type.
- Origin: Old handoff equipment that secured rescue stretchers to transport platforms just before flooding restarted in a Yongsan Line hangar. The authority that issued capture-release authorization has disappeared.
- Habitat and needs: It waits at stretcher-transfer ledges and in low loading rooms. It uses rubber straps, load-sensor pads, and power for its arm locks sparingly, and does not provide medical treatment itself.
- Life cycle and maintenance: When a strap splits, it spreads both arms in an out-of-service posture. If a maintainer passes off an old strap as new, it may drop the load on the next lift.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It takes heat sources marked by rank-and-file search units first. Struggling is recorded not as a wish to escape but as unstable cargo vibration, causing the straps to tighten further.
- Escalation: At stage 1, it collects empty stretchers. At stage 2, a person''s backpack catches in its arms; at stage 3, delays in release approval turn the entire transfer ledge into a capture queue.
- Combat and response: Transfer the person''s weight to an independent stretcher to unload the sensor, then release the outer clasp. Attacking the wrist joint transmits the impact to the person being held.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Handing storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses to the maintenance engineers lets the station watch borrow this unit''s work one shift at a time. A captured and repaired swarm becomes a search-light drone swarm. Entrust it with rescue assistance only if a human handoff worker checks the scene and new straps are provided. Ask the people being rescued, too, how far the convenience of treating people like secure cargo should be allowed to go.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0047"></a>
<a id="g25e10"></a>

### G25E10 · Twin-Compartment Carrier Ant

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: common ecology type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | General line |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Twin-Cell Cargo Ant was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The general line role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: Two large supply compartments hang on either side of its elongated ant-shaped abdomen. Its six broad feet cannot enter narrow search gaps but keep its cargo level on ramps.
- Origin: A large carrier that traveled between a supply depot outside the rescue site and the search entrance remained behind. It cannot erase the entrance number on an old delivery sheet in Mapo warehouse.
- Habitat and needs: It physically travels between gallery ramps and the Yongsan Line retrieval loading platform. Consumption of ankle bearings, cargo-compartment seals, and power-storage cylinders rises with the weight it carries.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It uses an internal scale to reduce the difference between the loads on its two sides, sometimes emptying one compartment. If the scale fails, it waits at the charging station with its body tilted.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It does not depart until the charging honeybee has delivered power. Because it trusts weight over packaging color, it can mistakenly swap containers of cooking oil and waste lubricating oil.
- Escalation: Stage 1 round trips with empty compartments continue, and at stage 2 citizens'' supplies pile up at the wrong entrance. At stage 3, carriers traveling against the flow block the uphill evacuation route.
- Combat and response: After checking the contents, balance the test weights in both compartments and hang the delivery-complete plate at the actual entrance. Overturning the chassis spills unsealed containers onto the ramp.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch cuts the lights in the gallery, covers stoves and heaters, and lowers the shutter, it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. A person can label each cargo item''s contents and determine its share of transport. Ownership of inventory entrusted to the swarm by a community must also be recognized at its destination.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0051"></a>
<a id="g25e11"></a>

### G25E11 · Scaled-Wing Sweeper

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: regional variant

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Local adaptation |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Scale-Wing Sweeper was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The local adaptation role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A butterfly-shaped reconnaissance flyer with reflective measuring tabs layered onto two pairs of thin wings. Alternating short flights with wall landings, it scans ceiling gaps that walking search units cannot see.
- Origin: A flying craft used for crack-observation experiments on the Yongsan Line was incorporated into the search line because it shared their return stand. Its original experiment-termination condition disappeared with the sealed documents.
- Habitat and needs: It spreads its wings only in test rooms with high ceilings. It needs membrane material, tiny rotary shafts, and a light battery; in narrow, waterlogged galleries, it lands and travels on foot.
- Life cycle and maintenance: If contamination of its left and right reflective tabs differs too much, it folds its wings and becomes a wall reference point. It cannot take off again without a replacement membrane.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It reports the locations of upper cracks but does not certify load-bearing safety. If it reads a gap reflected in glass as a real opening, search requests accumulate for the inside of the wall.
- Escalation: At stage 1, fragments of light waver across the ceiling; at stage 2, search routes shift upward. At stage 3, repeated sorties take charging turns away from survivors on the floor.
- Combat and response: Cover glass reflections with matte cloth and bring it down onto a nearby calibration plate. Tearing its wings to make it fall loses the reference coordinates as well.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Cutting the lights in the gallery and covering heaters is enough to make this regional type lose its bearings. A captured and repaired swarm becomes a search-light drone swarm. Supply light batteries in exchange for ceiling-observation data. A decision then follows: search for more unseen gaps or divert power to people already found.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0055"></a>
<a id="g25e12"></a>

### G25E12 · Iron-Shell Barricade Beetle

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: guardian

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Protection · cover |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Iron-Shell Screen Beetle was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The protection · cover role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A passage-protection unit with two interlocking beetle-shaped armor plates rising from its back. When it folds its legs inward, it becomes a small shelter canopy beneath falling debris.
- Origin: It began as a mobile canopy that caught fragments while pre-Blackout rescue teams passed through. A falling-debris alarm left at Mapo protection test site remains active after restart.
- Habitat and needs: It guards gallery ledges beneath damaged ceilings. It wears through armor connecting pins and cushioning blocks and cannot repeatedly open and close its heavy cover without a charging line.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It cannot straighten dented armor, so it flips the plates over and uses their reinforced inner faces. When both faces are crushed, the protected clearance drops and it stops people from passing.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It closes its cover in response to a falling-debris signal relayed by the cricket. If tapping on a distant wall is transmitted like a real fragment strike, it traps passersby inside.
- Escalation: Stage 1 is unexplained trembling of the cover. At stage 2, frequent closures delay rescue; at stage 3, the lock turns the protected space into a low compartment with no way out.
- Combat and response: Set support wedges beneath the cover before disconnecting the external impact sensor. An attack that presses down on it with weight also crushes the people it protects.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Before anyone gets close, the station watch must cut the lights in the gallery, cover stoves and heaters, and lower the shutter. Forcing a capture yields storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses but costs maintenance engineers their safety. The Arc can check the signal and switch the swarm on and off. It will maintain the protective cover if given real falling-debris inspections and a share of cushioning material. There is a cost to deciding whether scarce armor plates should go on residents'' roofs or remain with the mobile shelter.
- Scenario connections: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0059"></a>
<a id="g25e13"></a>

### G25E13 · Great-Resonance Relay Cricket

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: aging-stage type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Aged variant |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Great-Echo Relay Cricket was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The aged variant role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A large cricket-shaped unit with a tall resonance pillar on its back and its two hind legs hooked into floor rings. Its remaining function is to synchronize the timing of several small relay units rather than to move.
- Origin: A pre-Blackout main relay that coordinated searches across a wide area lost half its pillar during a long dormancy. When Mapo restored power, it released old response records all at once.
- Habitat and needs: It does not leave intersections of solid walls that can carry sound over long distances. It uses tension bolts, resonance panels, and substantial docking power, but cannot bridge the same distance through open air.
- Life cycle and maintenance: Fatigue cracks in the pillar cause the highest frequencies to disappear first. Small relay units can be connected to assist it, but they cannot fully restore its original bandwidth.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It adjusts only the order of responses between branches; it cannot change the mother relay tower’s work zones. Different calls merge into a single low note, creating spurious rescue requests.
- Escalation: At stage 1, it replays old calls; at stage 2, responses from every branch are delayed. At stage 3, unending response collisions bring departure confirmations to a halt.
- Engagement and response: Fit a test damping band to the resonance pillar and check each branch line by wire. Toppling the pillar tears out the wall anchors with it.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Because its service life is nearly over, capturing it and stripping storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses is the cheapest course. A captured and repaired swarm becomes a search-light drone swarm. It can be assigned only low-band emergency communications, with the rest handed over to human liaisons. The maintenance labor required to preserve the old relay must be weighed against faster new equipment.
- Scenario links: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0063"></a>
<a id="g25e14"></a>

### G25E14 · White-Jawed Cleaning Colony

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: common ecology type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | General line |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The White-Jaw Cleaning Swarm was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The general line role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: The front jaws of these small termite-shaped cleaners hold crumb brushes instead of chewing blades. When the collection pouch beneath the pale shell fills, the abdomen hangs lower.
- Origin: Preprocessing equipment that cleaned wiring grooves on the Yongsan Line test track responded to a search system’s inspection-start signal. Its table for distinguishing organic contaminants from household materials is outdated.
- Habitat and needs: They follow dust-filled cable grooves and gaps in Mapo insulation. They need brush fibers and collection pouches; they do not consume the debris they gather for power.
- Life cycle and maintenance: They empty full pouches at designated collection bins. Without a bin, they move less and maintain their numbers by exchanging damaged brushes rather than making new units.
- Behavior and hierarchy: They follow the clean groove edge left by the unit ahead and widen it. They also read cable-protective fibers and pieces of residents’ blankets as material to remove.
- Escalation: At stage 1, brush marks turn the floor white; at stage 2, insulation disappears. At stage 3, exposed wiring overlaps, making the inspection gallery itself an electrocution hazard.
- Engagement and response: Lead them along a detachable cleaning groove to a collection bin. Dousing them with toxic cleaner only contaminates the surfaces civilians walk on; it does not empty their pouches.
- Negotiation and moral choice: If the station watch cuts the lights in the gallery, covers stoves and heaters, and lowers the shutter, it does not come close. If it is captured and stripped of storage cells, precision motors, and optical lenses it stops, and the number in this district falls by that much. Set cleaning boundaries and waste ownership before assigning them work. Someone’s winter insulation must not be declared waste just to obtain clean cables.
- Scenario links: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0067"></a>
<a id="g25e15"></a>

### G25E15 · Honeycomb Charging Rack

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: environmental stronghold type

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Strongpoint control |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Honeycomb Charging Rack was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The strongpoint control role applied only within a fixed work zone, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A honeycomb-shaped rack whose hexagonal charging sockets cover a wall. It has only short legs underneath for lowering faulty batteries and cannot move itself to another room.
- Origin: A battery-exchange wall for pre-Blackout search units remained on an emergency line in the Yongsan Line hangar. After power was restored, old search priorities took precedence over civilian electricity needs.
- Habitat and needs: It is fixed to a live distribution line in a dry charging room. It uses electricity, insulating partitions, and fuses; however many sockets are empty, it cannot supply batteries unless they arrive from outside.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It isolates an overheated cell with a physical shutter and reduces the charging rate of neighboring cells. Melted contacts do not recover when a unit is docked, so the number of usable sockets shrinks.
- Behavior and hierarchy: Bee-shaped units take batteries from it but do not command the rack as a whole. If its internal thermometer sticks at a low reading, batteries in dangerous cells are issued in the normal sequence too.
- Escalation: Stage 1 is charging lights on empty cells. At stage 2, civilian power distribution falls; at stage 3, an overheating shutter physically pushes the access inspection door shut and locks it.
- Engagement and response: Reduce the load cell by cell, then use an independent thermometer to remove only safe batteries. Breaking the entire wall brings the terminals of different batteries into contact.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Because this fixture cannot be moved, the station watch lights the gallery only at set hours, covers stoves and heaters, insulates the walls to cut leaking heat, and keeps people beyond a set distance. The Arc can check the signal and switch the swarm on and off. The civilian lighting share and the swarm’s charging share can be allocated on the distribution chart. Keep observers present so that neither side can monopolize the charging-room key in the name of safety.
- Scenario links: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0071"></a>
<a id="g25e16"></a>

### G25E16 · Mother Echo Tower

- Source batch: M040
- Former role class: key unit

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Strategic encounter |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The Mother Echo Tower was a machine chassis type differentiated from the G25 common ecology. The strategic encounter role applied only at the independent-unit scope, and stood in for neither the whole swarm''s ecological order nor human command. |

- Role and body: A relay tower shaped like a long ant queen’s abdomen lies in the center of a maintenance room. Antennae on its head and ring-shaped terminals coordinate the order of calls across search zones.
- Origin: The final headcount device for a large-scale rescue drill never received a completion report because of the collapse. Its nickname, mother, comes not from a biological reproductive organ but from an old work schedule that is hard to read.
- Habitat and needs: It operates only in the connection room of Mapo sealed gallery. It needs cooling filters, recording modules, and distributed power; it has no facilities for producing new search units.
- Life cycle and maintenance: It tries to lock zones with faulty records one by one, leaving only valid calls. As the remaining schedule shrinks, searches concentrate in the same narrow zones, and it cannot restore the schedule without human approval.
- Behavior and hierarchy: It distributes start and return calls through small relay units but cannot directly see actual survivors. Its treatment of every nonresponse as a person still awaiting rescue is at the heart of the swarm’s fixation.
- Escalation: At stage 1, calls from decommissioned zones resume. At stage 2, charging and transport converge on those zones. At stage 3, a discrepancy in the final headcount suspends completion approval for every connected exit.
- Engagement and response: First the station watch switches off lamps and lowers the shutter to slow its approach. Compare a physical map of the zones with return records at an isolated terminal, sealing nonexistent addresses first. Cutting only the central power leaves remote relays repeating the last call, so each branch must be shut down separately.
- Negotiation and moral choice: Several station watches must cut the lights in the gallery and cover the heaters at the same hour and get an Arc signal check before anyone can approach. The Arc can check the signal and switch the swarm on and off. Stopping it is cheaper than capturing it. Decide whether to close unverified zones permanently or retain them for limited searches. Declaring the operation complete opens routes for current residents, but leaves responsibility for abandoning the records of people not yet found.
- Scenario links: G25-SC1, G25-SC2, G25-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0075"></a>

## Habitat record

From a distance, the lights on the floor looked like a dotted line showing the way to evacuate.

Up close, under each dot were six legs and a small cargo bin, and two lines of units flowed in opposite directions along the broken cable shelves.

The Lantern Ant that first woke in the transmission underground gallery of Mapo did not move in order to light the dark.

The lead line of ant types measured loads and the rear line carried empty relief capsules, while the mantis-type vanguard arms secured the width of the double line.

When the cricket-type transmitters clinging near the ceiling sent short call tones, the lights of the next charging stands came on one after another.

This line was a biomimetic swarm robot that had handled disaster-terrain exploration and automatic relief delivery before the Great Blackout.

It remained dormant in the sealed gallery of Mapo and on the laboratory test track of the Yongsan Line, and on the day later generations partly restored the transmission distribution and test equipment, it took its last training table for a real disaster order and woke.

The intent of its makers and the form of its higher instructions could not be read in full.

Some markers made a person a recipient and others turned the same person into a moving obstacle, but there was no original ledger to explain the difference.

So they are treated less as new weapons than as the leftover motions of old machines whose makers are gone.

Its present behavior was not a politics it had built for itself but the fossilized search sectors and delivery order from before the sealing.

The swarm''s perception was low and dense.

The ant types read the slope of the floor, the pressure on each leg, nearby heat, and changes in breathing.

The mantis types gauged the weight of debris by joint torque, and the cricket types confirmed the next sector by the return time of acoustic pulses and short-range replies.

They could not see faces or names.

Hot pipes were marked as survivors lying down, generators that trembled regularly became rescue beacons, and a person carrying an old reflector could be classified as a moving target on the test track.

Even when Lab Cleanroom corrected the sensor map, final passage opened only after the station duty officer matched the floor loads to the evacuation roster.

Having many units did not mean they increased without end.

Power was tied to the sealed vault''s batteries and induction charging plates, and when a single relay fell silent, the double lines beyond it returned to their last checkpoint.

They could move the legs and lenses of broken units into the same slots, but could not make new bodies.

The distribution core of a sector kept nearly half its power for the return, and when reserves ran low the mantis types folded their arms first.

The more precision motors the maintenance-engineer recovery crew took, the smaller the search radius became, and the more charging slots citizens gave up, the less power was left for the other equipment in the transmission gallery.

The resource ledger of this ecology was written not in numbers of units but in relayable distance and the number of cargo bins returned.

The double lines that emerged first measured the floor in two rows, and in the spreading stage advanced their transmitters one sector at a time.

When route markers conflicted, they stalled, circling the same loop.

At that moment, if the correct recipient markers, empty cargo bins, and transmitter silence times were presented, people could rewrite part of the delivery queue.

Breaking it up was not an event of smashing every unit but a procedure of isolating the main transmitter and separating the route ledgers by charging stand.

The choice of keeping one line of lights for rescue while sealing the other line was possible.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0104"></a>

### G25-SC1 · The Beacon Crying Inside the Wall

Behind a wall of Mapo gallery, a three-beat rescue call repeated, and the lead line of ant types began tearing at the cable shelves.

While it was not yet confirmed whether this was a real survival signal or an old test transmitter, the maintenance-engineer recovery crew tried to preserve the wall, and Lab Cleanroom caught a faint trace of breathing heat once.

The people on site had to either cut the call sector by sector with sound-absorbing cloth and send in only four contact-search units, or have the mantis types open the wall and accept the loss of the transmission cable.

Sealing it wrongly meant abandoning the chance of rescue, and cutting in haste meant one channel of Mapo''s outside contact disappeared.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0109"></a>

### G25-SC2 · The Relief Capsules That Did Not Return

Medical capsules on the relief list from beyond the cordon outside Seoul piled up in front of mock recipients on the laboratory test track instead of at the temporary clinic on the Yongsan Line.

The distribution core recorded boxes with intact seals as mission success and rejected the citizens'' new markers.

The people on site could offer the maintenance engineers'' antenna plates in exchange and get a new delivery table approved for just one shift, recover the existing cargo bins and carry them by hand, or physically move the test receiving point close to the clinic.

The first choice used up spare parts, the second delayed the arrival of the injured, and the third made the swarm patrol the clinic zone from then on as if it were its own test ground.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G25-heading-0114"></a>

### G25-SC3 · Two Lines of Lockdown Lights

At the underground junction linking the Mapo and the Yongsan Line, three cricket-type units gave off different return tones, and the double lines tangled into a ring.

The mantis-type vanguards tore out railings and barrier doors to turn the loop into a passage, and trapped citizens could not get out of the circle of light.

The people on site had to create precise silent intervals between the transmitters and send the lines back to the charging stands one at a time.

Destroying the main transmitter made an opening at once, but the small units that lost their bearings scattered into the cable layers, and cutting all power stopped the transmission and the laboratory safety monitoring as well.

The ending that kept one double line for rescue preserved the ability to search for survivors in narrow debris zones, at the price of carrying slow inspection and charge allocation throughout the campaign.
